.1786*(\u221a3/2) -0.4226*(0.5774) +0.3333*(1/2)\n\nCompute each term:\n\nFirst term:\u22480.1786*0.866\u22480.1547\n\nSecond term:\u22480.4226*0.5774\u22480.244\n\nThird term:\u22480.3333*0.5\u22480.1667\n\nTherefore, f(x_c)\u22480.1547 -0.244 +0.1667\u22480.1547+0.1667=0.3214 -0.244\u22480.0774>0\n\nSo even though \u03b8=\u03c0/6 is within (\u03c0/12,5\u03c0/12), which was supposed to satisfy the inequality, the minimum is still positive. Wait, but according to our earlier conclusion, \u03b8=\u03c0/6 should be in the solution set. But according to the initial steps, we had \u03b8 \u2208 (\u03c0/12,5\u03c0/12) as solutions. But \u03b8=\u03c0/6 is 30\u00b0, which is 2\u03c0/12, so between \u03c0/12 and5\u03c0/12, so it is included. However, according to the critical point calculation, even at \u03b8=\u03c0/6, the minimum is positive. Therefore, seems like our solution \u03b8 \u2208 (\u03c0/12,5\u03c0/12) is correct.\n\nWait, but let's take \u03b8=\u03c0/12 (15 degrees) and see what happens.\n\n\u03b8=\u03c0/12:\n\nsin\u03b8=sin(15\u00b0)= (\u221a6 -\u221a2)/4\u22480.2588\n\ncos\u03b8=cos(15\u00b0)= (\u221a6 +\u221a2)/4\u22480.9659\n\nCompute f(x)=x\u00b2 cos\u03b8 -x(1 -x) + (1 -x)^2 sin\u03b8\n\nCompute at x_c:\n\nx_c=(1 + 2 sin\u03b8)/(2(cos\u03b8 + sin\u03b8 +1))\n\nCompute numerator:1 +2*0.2588\u22481.5176\n\nDenominator:2*(0.9659 +0.2588 +1)=2*(2.2247)\u22484.4494\n\nx_c\u22481.5176/4.4494\u22480.341\n\nCompute f(x_c):\n\nx=0.341\n\nx\u00b2\u22480.1163\n\n(1 -x)\u00b2\u22480.659^2\u22480.434\n\nf(x)=0.1163*0.9659 -0.341*(1 -0.341) +0.434*0.2588\n\nFirst term:0.1163*0.9659\u22480.1124\n\nSecond term:0.341*0.659\u22480.2247\n\nThird term:0.434*0.2588\u22480.1123\n\nThus, f(x_c)=0.1124 -0.2247 +0.1123\u22480.1124+0.1123=0.2247 -0.2247=0.0000\u22480\n\nTherefore, f(x_c)=0. So at \u03b8=\u03c0/12, the minimum is zero, which violates the strict inequality. Therefore, \u03b8=\u03c0/12 is excluded.\n\nSimilarly, at \u03b8=5\u03c0/12:\n\n\u03b8=75\u00b0, sin\u03b8=sin(7