ce \\(\\{b_n\\}\\) defined as\n    \\[\n    b_n = a_n - n\n    \\]\n    Given that \\(1 < a_1 < a_2 < \\ldots < a_n < \\ldots\\), it is clear that \\(b_n\\) represents the difference between \\(a_n\\) and \\(n\\).\n\n2. **Non-decreasing Nature of \\(b_n\\)**:\n    We know the original sequence \\(\\{a_n\\}\\) is strictly increasing:\n    \\[\n    a_{n+1} > a_n\n    \\]\n    Therefore,\n    \\[\n    b_{n+1} = a_{n+1} - (n+1) > a_n - n = b_n\n    \\]\n    Hence, the sequence \\(\\{b_n\\}\\) is non-decreasing:\n    \\[\n    b_{n+1} \\geq b_n\n    \\]\n\n3. **Rewriting the Problem Condition**:\n    Using the given problem condition \\(a_{n + a_n} = 2a_n\\), we can express it in terms of \\(\\{b_n\\}\\):\n    \\[\n    a_{n + a_n} = 2a_n\n    \\]\n    Substituting \\(a_n = b_n + n\\):\n    \\[\n    a_{n + (b_n + n)} = 2(b_n + n)\n    \\]\n    Simplifying:\n    \\[\n    a_{2n + b_n} = 2b_n + 2n\n    \\]\n    And using the definition \\(b_{2n + b_n} = a_{2n + b_n} - (2n + b_n)\\):\n    \\[\n    b_{2n + b_n} = 2(b_n + n) - (2n + b_n) = b_n\n    \\]\n\n4. **Concluding Constancy of \\(b_n\\)**:\n    The equation \\(b_{2n + b_n} = b_n\\) indicates that \\(b_n\\) repeats its value within the sequence indefinitely.\n    Since \\(\\{b_n\\}\\) is non-decreasing and takes an infinite number of values, it must be constant.\n\n5. **Existence of a Constant \\(C\\)**:\n    Since \\(\\{b_n\\}\\) is constant, let \\(b_n = C\\) for some constant \\(C\\).\n    Therefore,\n    \\[\n    a_n - n = C \\quad \\forall n\n    \\]\n    Which can be rewritten as:\n    \\[\n    a_n = n + C\n    \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nThus, the elements of the sequence \\(\\{a_n\\}\\) can be written as \\(a_n = n + C\\) for some constant natural number \\(C\\). \n\\[\n\\boxed{a_n = n + C}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "At lunch, Abby, Bart, Carl, Dana, and Evan share a pizza divided radially into 16 slices. Each one takes one slice of pizza un