dx * x'.\n\nBut x' = y z = sqrt(x\u00b2 - x0\u00b2) * sqrt(x\u00b2 - D).\n\nThus, dz/dt = x sqrt(x\u00b2 - x0\u00b2) = dz/dx * sqrt(x\u00b2 - x0\u00b2) * sqrt(x\u00b2 - D).\n\nTherefore:\n\ndz/dx = [x sqrt(x\u00b2 - x0\u00b2)] / [sqrt(x\u00b2 - x0\u00b2) sqrt(x\u00b2 - D))] = x / sqrt(x\u00b2 - D).\n\nThus, dz = x dx / sqrt(x\u00b2 - D).\n\nIntegrate both sides:\n\n\u222b dz = \u222b x dx / sqrt(x\u00b2 - D).\n\nLeft side: z + C1.\n\nRight side: sqrt(x\u00b2 - D) + C2.\n\nSo z = sqrt(x\u00b2 - D) + C.\n\nAt t=0, x =x0, z =z0. So:\n\nz0 = sqrt(x0\u00b2 - D) + C.\n\nBut D =x0\u00b2 - z0\u00b2, so sqrt(x0\u00b2 - D) = sqrt(z0\u00b2) = z0.\n\nThus, z0 = z0 + C => C=0.\n\nTherefore, z = sqrt(x\u00b2 - D) = sqrt(x\u00b2 - (x0\u00b2 - z0\u00b2)) = sqrt(x\u00b2 -x0\u00b2 + z0\u00b2).\n\nBut this seems complicated. Maybe this approach isn't helpful.\n\nAlternatively, consider the derivative of x. From x' = y z = sqrt(x\u00b2 - x0\u00b2) sqrt(x\u00b2 - D). This seems messy. Maybe there's a better approach.\n\nAlternatively, notice that in Problem 2 and 3, the solutions blow up because the ODEs lead to a finite escape time. For example, in Problem 2, the solution involved sec t which blows up at \u03c0/2. In Problem 3, the solution blows up at t=-1. So in both cases, the solution exists only for a finite interval.\n\nTo show that in general, when two coordinates are non-zero, the solution blows up, we need to show that the solution cannot exist for all time, i.e., it has a finite escape time.\n\nOne method to show this is to demonstrate that the solution reaches infinity in finite time by analyzing the growth rate. For example, if the derivative of some quantity grows proportionally to the square of that quantity, then the solution can blow up in finite time.\n\nLet\u2019s consider the case where two coordinates are non-zero. Suppose x0 \u22600 and y0 \u22600, and z0=0. Then, as in Problem 2, the solution for z is tan t, which blows up at t=\u03c0/2. Alternatively, if x0 and z0 are non-zero with y0=0, then similar behavior.\n\nBut to generalize, suppose we have two coordinates non-zero. Let's assume, for example, x and y are non-zero, and z=0. Then z' = x y, which is non-zero, so z starts to increase. Then x' = y z and y' = x z. As z increases, the derivatives of x and y become larger, leading to even faster growth of x, y, and z. This positive feedback loop can lead to blow-up in finite time.\n\nAlternatively, consider the system's behavior. Let's suppose that x, y, z are all positive (for simplicity). Then, the derivatives x', y', z' are all positive, leading to increasing x, y, z. The rates of increase depend on the product of the other two variables, which are themselves increasing. This suggests that the variables grow faster than exponentially, leading to blow-up in finite time.\n\nTo formalize this, suppose that x, y, z are all increasing and positive. Let\u2019s make an ansatz that x, y, z all approach infinity as t approaches some finite T. Let\u2019s try to estimate the time to blow-up.\n\nAssume that x \u2248 y \u2248 z as they grow large. Then x' \u2248 x\u00b2, leading to dx/dt \u2248 x\u00b2, whose solution is x \u2248 1/(C - t), which blows up as t approaches C. This suggests finite-time blow-up.\n\nAlternatively, even if they are not equal, if two variables are growing, their product will drive the third variable, leading to all variables growing without bound.\n\nAlternatively, use the conserved quantities. For example, from Problem 2, we had x\u00b2 - z\u00b2 =1. If x and z are both growing, x \u2248 z, but x\u00b2 - z\u00b2=1 suggests they approach a hyperbola, but in reality, the parametrization shows that they can still blow up.\n\nAlternatively, use the fact that d/dt (x + y + z) = x' + y' + z' = y z + z x + x y. If x, y, z are all positive, this sum is positive and growing, leading to x + y + z blowing up.\n\nBut how to rigorously show that this happens in finite time? Maybe use a comparison theorem. Suppose we can show that, for example, x' \u2265 k x\u00b2 for some k >0, which would imply finite-time blow-up.\n\nSuppose two coordinates are non-zero. Let's say x and y are non-zero. Then z' = x y. If x and y are bounded below by some positive constants, then z' is bounded below, leading to z growing at least linearly. Then x' = y z and y' = x z would grow at least linearly, leading to x and y growing at least quadratically, which in turn makes z' grow quadratically, and so on. This super-linear growth suggests finite-time blow-up.\n\nAlternatively, consider the quantity S = x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2. Then dS/dt = 2x x' + 2y y' + 2z z' = 2x(y z) + 2y(z x) + 2z(x y) = 6xyz.\n\nIf we can relate S and xyz, perhaps we can find a differential inequality. For example, by AM \u2265 GM:\n\n(x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2)/3 \u2265 (x y z)^(2/3).\n\nBut S/3 \u2265 (xyz)^(2/3) => xyz \u2264 (S/3)^(3/2).\n\nThus, dS/dt = 6xyz \u2264 6 (S/3)^(3/2) = 6 * (3)^{-3/2} S^{3/2} = C S^{3/2}.\n\nSo dS/dt \u2264 C S^{3/2}.\n\nThis is a differential inequality: dS/dt \u2264 C S^{3/2}.\n\nThe solution to dS/dt = C S^{3/2} is S(t) = 1/( (C1 - (C/2) t)^2 ), which blows up as t approaches 2 C1 / C. Therefore, even with this inequality, S(t) would blow up in finite time if the inequality is tight. However, our inequality is dS/dt \u2264 C S^{3/2}, so the actual growth could be faster, leading to blow-up sooner.\n\nTherefore, this suggests that S(t) blows up in finite time. Hence, the distance d = sqrt(S) also blows up.\n\nBut this requires that xyz remains positive and significant. If two coordinates are non-zero and the third is zero, initially, then z starts to grow, making xyz positive, and thus dS/dt becomes positive, leading to growth. Once all coordinates are non-zero, the above analysis applies.\n\nTherefore, in general, if two coordinates are non-zero initially, the system will evolve such that all coordinates become non-zero and their products drive each other to infinity in finite time.\n\nAlternatively, consider specific cases. If two coordinates are non-zero, the third will start changing, leading to all three becoming non-zero. Then, using the conserved quantities, we can relate the variables. For example, take x\u00b2 - y\u00b2 = C1 and x\u00b2 - z\u00b2 = C2. If we can express y and z in terms of x, then substitute into the equation for x' and solve.\n\nFor example, let\u2019s assume x\u00b2 - y\u00b2 = C1 and x\u00b2 - z\u00b2 = C2. Then y = sqrt(x\u00b2 - C1), z = sqrt(x\u00b2 - C2). Then x' = y z = sqrt(x\u00b2 - C1) sqrt(x\u00b2 - C2).\n\nThis gives dx/dt = sqrt( (x\u00b2 - C1)(x\u00b2 - C2) ).\n\nThis is a separable equation. The time to blow-up can be found by integrating from x0 to infinity:\n\nT = \u222b_{x0}^\u221e dx / sqrt( (x\u00b2 - C1)(x\u00b2 - C2) ).\n\nIf this integral converges, then the solution blows up in finite time.\n\nThe convergence of the integral depends on the behavior as x approaches infinity. For large x, the integrand behaves like 1/x\u00b2. The integral of 1/x\u00b2 from some X to infinity converges, so the total integral converges. Therefore, T is finite, meaning x reaches infinity in finite time. Hence, the solution blows up.\n\nTherefore, this shows that if two coordinates are non-zero initially, leading to C1 and C2 being constants such that the integral converges, then the solution blows up in finite time.\n\nThus, in general, for any initial condition where two coordinates are non-zero, the system's solution will blow up in finite time, either in the past or future, causing the distance d to tend to infinity.", "deepseek_solution": "**1. If two coordinates are zero at \\( t = 0 \\), all derivatives vanish.**\n- Suppose \\( x_0 = y_0 = 0 \\). Then \\( x' = y z = 0 \\), \\( y' = z x = 0 \\), and \\( z' = x y = 0 \\). Hence, \\( (x, y, z) \\) remains stationary. Similar logic applies if any two coordinates are zero.\n\n**2. For \\( (x_0, y_0, z_0) = (1, 1, 0) \\):**\n- Symmetry implies \\( x(t) = y(t) \\). Substituting \\( x = y \\) into the equations gives:\n  \\[\n  x' = x z, \\quad z' = x^2.\n  \\]\n- Let \\( x = \\sec t \\), \\( z = \\tan t \\). Derivatives:\n  \\[\n  x' = \\sec t \\tan t = x z, \\quad z' = \\sec^2 t = x^2.\n  \\]\n- Verified by substitution, satisfying the initial conditions.\n\n**3. For \\( (x_0, y_0, z_0) = (1, 1, -1) \\):**\n- Assume \\( x = y = -z \\). Then:\n  \\[\n  x' = -x^2 \\implies \\frac{dx}{dt} = -x^2 \\implies x = \\frac{1}{1 + t}.\n  \\]\n- Hence, \\( z = -\\frac{1}{1 + t} \\), matching the proposed solution.\n\n**4. Blow-up for two non-zero coordinates:**\n- Assume two coordinates (e.g., \\( x_0, y_0 \\)) are non-zero. Then \\( z' = x y \\) becomes non-zero, driving \\( z \\). The system enters a positive feedback loop:\n  \\[\n  x' = y z, \\quad y' = x z, \\quad z' = x y.\n  \\]\n- The conserved quantities \\( x^2 - y^2 \\), \\( x^2 - z^2 \\), and \\( y^2 - z^2 \\) constrain the dynamics. Solving \\( x' = \\sqrt{(x^2 - C_1)(x^2 - C_2)} \\) leads to finite-time divergence as \\( x \\to \\infty \\), proven via integral convergence. Thus, \\( d \\to \\infty \\) at finite \\( t \\).\n\n**Final Answers**\n\n1. \\(\\boxed{\\text{The object remains stationary.}}\\)\n\n2. \\(\\boxed{(x, y, z) = (\\sec t, \\sec t, \\tan t)}\\)\n\n3. \\(\\boxed{(x, y, z) = \\left(\\frac{1}{1 + t}, \\frac{1}{1 + t}, -\\frac{1}{1 + t}\\right)}\\)\n\n4. \\(\\boxed{d \\to \\infty \\text{ at some finite } t}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\n#### Part 1: If two of the coordinates are zero, show that the object is stationary for all t.\n\n1. Assume without loss of generality that \\( (x_0, y_0, z_0) = (a, b, 0) \\).\n\n2. The equations of motion are:\n   \\[\n   x' = yz, \\quad y' = zx, \\quad z' = xy\n   \\]\n   \n3. Substituting \\( z_0 = 0 \\):\n   \\[\n   x' = yz = y \\cdot 0 = 0\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y' = zx = x \\cdot 0 = 0\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   z' = xy = x \\cdot y\n   \\]\n\n4. Given \\( x' = 0 \\) and \\( y' = 0 \\), it follows that \\( x(t) = x_0 \\) and \\( y(t) = y_0 \\) are constants.\n\n5. Now, \\( z' = xy = \\text{constant} \\cdot \\text{constant} = 0 \\), hence \\( z(t) = z_0 = 0 \\).\n\n6. Therefore, the object does not move, as all coordinates are constant.\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\(\\boxed{\\text{The object is stationary for all time } t.}\\)\n\n#### Part 2: If \\((x_0, y_0, z_0) = (1, 1, 0)\\), show that \\((x, y, z) = (\\sec t, \\sec t, \\tan t)\\).\n  \n1. We need to verify that \\((\\sec t, \\sec t, \\tan t)\\) satisfies the equations of motion.\n\n2. Recall the derivatives for the given functions:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{d}{dt} (\\sec t) = \\sec t \\tan t \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{d}{dt} (\\tan t) = \\sec^2 t\n   \\]\n\n3. Substitute \\((x, y, z) = (\\sec t, \\sec t, \\tan t)\\):\n\n   - For \\(x' = yz\\):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{d}{dt} (\\sec t) = \\sec t \\tan t\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     yz = \\sec t \\cdot \\tan t = \\sec t \\tan t\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\text{Thus } x' = yz\n     \\]\n\n   - For \\(y' = zx\\):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{d}{dt} (\\sec t) = \\sec t \\tan t\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     zx = \\tan t \\cdot \\sec t = \\sec t \\tan t\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\text{Thus } y' = zx\n     \\]\n\n   - For \\(z' = xy\\):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{d}{dt} (\\tan t) = \\sec^2 t\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     xy = \\sec t \\cdot \\sec t = \\sec^2 t\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\text{Thus } z' = xy\n     \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nSince all conditions are satisfied, we have \\((x, y, z) = (\\sec t, \\sec t, \\tan t)\\).\n\\(\\boxed{(x, y, z) = (\\sec t, \\sec t, \\tan t)}\\)\n\n#### Part 3: If \\((x_0, y_0, z_0) = (1, 1, -1)\\), show that \\((x, y, z) = \\left(\\frac{1}{1 + t}, \\frac{1}{1 + t}, \\frac{-1}{1 + t}\\right)\\).\n\n1. We need to verify that \\(\\left(\\frac{1}{1 + t}, \\frac{1}{1 + t}, \\frac{-1}{1 + t}\\right)\\) satisfies the equations of motion.\n\n2. Recall the derivatives for the given functions:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{1}{1 + t}\\right) = -\\frac{1}{(1 + t)^2}\n   \\]\n\n3. Substitute \\((x, 