rmutation matrices, each rook in a row is in a unique column, so the two rooks in each row are in different columns, but there are no other rooks between them in their row. Therefore, each pair of rooks in the same row (two per row) form an attacking pair, and similarly for columns. Therefore, in such an arrangement, each row contributes one attacking pair (since two rooks per row), and each column contributes one attacking pair (since two rooks per column), leading to 8+8=16 attacking pairs total.\n\nTherefore, this arrangement is valid and results in 16 attacking pairs. Since any deviation from two rooks per row or column would increase the sum, as shown in the earlier example, this must be the minimal possible number. Therefore, the answer is 16.", "deepseek_solution": "The minimum number of attacking pairs of rooks is achieved by distributing the rooks evenly across the board. Each of the 8 rows and 8 columns must contain exactly 2 rooks. In this configuration:\n\n- **Rows**: Each row with 2 rooks contributes \\(2 - 1 = 1\\) attacking pair. With 8 rows, this totals \\(8 \\times 1 = 8\\) pairs.\n- **Columns**: Similarly, each column with 2 rooks contributes \\(2 - 1 = 1\\) attacking pair. With 8 columns, this also totals \\(8 \\times 1 = 8\\) pairs.\n\nAdding these together gives \\(8 + 8 = 16\\) attacking pairs. This arrangement is feasible, such as placing two non-overlapping permutation matrices, ensuring no rook blocks another within the same row or column.\n\n**Answer:** The minimum number of attacking pairs is \\(\\boxed{16}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "### Problem:\nOn an $8 \\times 8$ international chessboard, each of the 16 squares contains one rook. What is the minimum number of pairs of rooks that can mutually attack each other (lying on the same row or column, with no other pieces in between)?\n\n\n1. **Determine Rooks in Each Row:**  \n   If a row contains \\( a \\) rooks, then the number of mutually attacking pairs of rooks in that row is \\( \\binom{a}{2} = \\frac{a(a-1)}{2} \\).\n\n2. **Calculate Minimum Pairs in Each Row:**  \n   Since the board has 8 rows and 16 rooks, each rook needs to be placed somewhere, at the minimum \\(2\\) rooks per row in 8 rows (1 rook per row would only use 8 rooks).\n\n3. **Pairs in Straight-Line Direction (Row-wise):**  \n   With at least \\(2\\) rooks per row, the minimum number of pairs is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Pairs per row} = \\binom{2}{2} + \\binom{2}{2} + \\cdots + \\binom{2}{2} = 1 + 1 + \\cdots + 1 = 8 \\text{ pairs}\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the minimum pairs in rows are \\( 8 \\).\n\n4. **Pairs in Vertical Direction (Column-wise):**\n   By the same logic applied to columns, the minimum number of pairs of rooks in each column will also be \\( 8 \\). That is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Pairs per column} = \\binom{2}{2} + \\binom{2}{2} + \\cdots + \\binom{2}{2} = 1 + 1 + \\cdots + 1 = 8 \\text{ pairs}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Total Minimum Pairs:**\n   The total number of minimum mutually attacking pairs is the sum of pairs in rows and columns:\n   \\[\n   8 \\text{ (row-wise pairs)} + 8 \\text{ (column-wise pairs)} = 16 \\text{ pairs}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Configuration Demonstrating Minimum Pairs:**\n   One possible configuration to achieve this is placing all the rooks on two principal diagonals of the board e.g., placing \\( 8 \\) rooks from \\( (1,1) \\) to \\( (8,8) \\) and another 8 rooks from \\( (1,8) \\) to \\( (8,1) \\), ensuring that no two pieces are placed in the same row or column more than twice.\n\nConclusion: \n\\[\n\\boxed{16} \\text{ (pairs of rooks that can mutually attack each other)}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "The sequence \\(\\{a_{n}\\}\\) satisfies \\(a_{1}=6\\), and for any positive integer \\(n\\), \\(a_{n+1}+n+1=2(a_{n}+1)\\). What is the units digit of \\(a_{1}+a_{2}+\\cdots+a_{2022}\\)?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the units digit of the sum a\u2081 + a\u2082 + ... + 