n\nWe find the units digit by computing \\(S \\mod 10\\):\n1. \\(2^{2022} \\mod 10\\): The units digit of \\(2^n\\) cycles every 4. Since \\(2022 \\mod 4 = 2\\), \\(2^{2022} \\equiv 4 \\mod 10\\). Thus, \\(5(2^{2022} - 1) \\equiv 5(4 - 1) \\equiv 15 \\equiv 5 \\mod 10\\).\n2. \\(\\frac{2022 \\cdot 2023}{2} \\mod 10\\): Compute \\(2022 \\mod 20 = 2\\) and \\(2023 \\mod 20 = 3\\). Their product is \\(6 \\mod 20\\), divided by 2 gives \\(3 \\mod 10\\).\n\nAdding these results:\n\\[ 5 + 3 \\equiv 8 \\mod 10 \\]\n\nThe units digit of \\(a_1 + a_2 + \\cdots + a_{2022}\\) is \\(\\boxed{8}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Given the recurrence relation \\( a_{n+1} + n + 1 = 2(a_{n} + 1) \\), with \\( a_1 = 6 \\), we need to find the sum \\( a_1 + a_2 + \\cdots + a_{2022} \\) and the unit digit of that sum. Let's rewrite the given recurrence relation:\n   \\[\n   a_{n+1} + n + 1 = 2(a_{n} + 1)\n   \\]\n   Simplify this to:\n   \\[\n   a_{n+1} + n + 1 = 2a_{n} + 2\n   \\]\n   Rearranging terms leads to:\n   \\[\n   a_{n+1} = 2a_{n} - n + 1\n   \\]\n\n2.  To solve this recurrence relation, we try to find a particular solution of the form \\(a_{n} = P + Q \\cdot n + R \\cdot 2^n \\). Upon substituting and simplifying, we identify:\n    \\[\n    k = -1, \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = 0\n    \\]\n    Using this form, the recurrence relation simplifies to:\n    \\[\n    a_{n+1} - (n + 1) = 2(a_{n} - n)\n    \\]\n    Finally, set \\(a_{n} - n = c \\cdot 2^{n-1}\\), where \\(c\\) is a constant. Since \\(a_1 = 6\\):\n    \\[\n    a_1 - 1 = 2^0 \\cdot c \\implies 6 - 1 = c \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad c = 5\n    \\]\n    Therefore,\n    \\[\n    a_{n} - n = 5 \\cdot 2^{n-1} \\implies a_{n} = 5 \\cdot 2^{n-1} + n\n    \\]\n\n3. Now, to find the sum \\(a_1 + a_2 + \\cdots + a_{2022}\\):\n    \\[\n    a_1 + a_2 + \\cdots + a_{2022} = \\sum_{n=1}^{2022} \\left(5 \\cdot 2^{n-1} + n\\right)\n    \\]\n   This can be split into two sums:\n    \\[\n    \\sum_{n=1}^{2022} \\left(5 \\cdot 2^{n-1}\\right) + \\sum_{n=1}^{2022} n\n    \\]\n    First sum, using geometric series:\n    \\[\n    \\sum_{n=1}^{2022} 5 \\cdot 2^{n-1} = 5 \\left( \\sum_{n=0}^{2021} 2^n \\right) = 5 \\left( 2^{2022} - 1 \\right)\n    \\]\n\n   Second sum, using the formula for the sum of first \\(n\\) positive integers:\n    \\[\n    \\sum_{n=1}^{2022} n = \\frac{2022 \\cdot (2022 + 1)}{2} = \\frac{2022 \\cdot 2023}{2} = 2023 \\cdot 1011\n    \\]\n\n4. Summing these two results:\n    \\[\n    a_1 + a_2 + \\cdots + a_{2022} = 5 (2^{2022} - 1) + 2023 \\cdot 1011\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    = 5 \\cdot 2^{2022} - 5 + 2023 \\cdot 1011\n    \\]\n\n5. We need to find the unit digit (ones place) of this sum. Determine the unit digit of each component separately:\n   - \\(5 \\cdot 2^{2022}: \\text{Note that the unit digit of } 2^{n} \\text{ cycles every 4 powers } (2, 4, 8, 6)\\). Since \\(2022 \\div 4\\) gives remainder 2, the unit digit of \\(2^{2022}\\) is 4. Thus, the unit digit of \\(5 \\cdot 2^{2022}\\) is \\((5 \\cdot 4 = 20) \\rightarrow 0\\)\n   - \\(2023 \\cdot 1011: \\text{ unit digits of } 2023 \\text{ and } 1011 \\text{ are 3 and 1, respectively. } 3 \\cdot 1 = 3\\)\n\n6. Combining unit digits:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Unit digit of } 5 \\cdot 2^{2022} = 0\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Unit digit of } 2023 \\cdot 1011 = 3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   5 \\cdot 2^{2022} + 2023 \\cdot 1011 = 0 + 3 - 5 \\equiv -2 \\equiv 8 \\quad (\\text{mod} \\; 10)\n   \\]\n\n7. Therefore, the unit digit of the sum \\(a_1 + a_2 + \\cdots + a_{2022}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{8}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "On a circle, \\(4n\\) points are marked and colored alternately in red and blue. The points of each color are divided into pairs, and the points of each pair are connected by segments of the same color. Prove that if no three segments intersect at a single point, then there are at least \\(n\\) intersections of red segments with blue segments.", "dee