0b3(2x)\n\nSince sec(2x) > 0 and tan\u00b2(2x) \u2265 0, the second derivative is positive. Therefore, f(x) = sec(2x) is convex on (0, \u03c0/4).\n\nNow, check the convexity of g(x) = csc x = 1/sin x on (0, \u03c0/4).\n\nFirst derivative: g'(x) = -cot x csc x\n\nSecond derivative: g''(x) = cot\u00b2 x csc x + csc\u00b3 x\n\nBecause derivative of -cot x csc x is:\n\nLet me compute step by step:\n\ng'(x) = -cot x csc x\n\nThen, g''(x) = - [ d/dx (cot x) * csc x + cot x * d/dx (csc x) ]\n\nCompute derivatives:\n\nd/dx (cot x) = -csc\u00b2 x\n\nd/dx (csc x) = -cot x csc x\n\nThus,\n\ng''(x) = - [ (-csc\u00b2 x) * csc x + cot x * (-cot x csc x) ]\n\n= - [ -csc\u00b3 x - cot\u00b2 x csc x ]\n\n= csc\u00b3 x + cot\u00b2 x csc x\n\nFactor out csc x:\n\n= csc x (cot\u00b2 x + csc\u00b2 x)\n\nBut cot\u00b2 x + 1 = csc\u00b2 x, so cot\u00b2 x + csc\u00b2 x = 2 csc\u00b2 x - 1. Wait, no:\n\nWait, cot\u00b2 x + csc\u00b2 x = (csc\u00b2 x - 1) + csc\u00b2 x = 2 csc\u00b2 x - 1. Hmm, but this might not matter. The point is, since csc x > 0 in (0, \u03c0/4), and cot\u00b2 x + csc\u00b2 x is positive, then g''(x) > 0. Therefore, g(x) = csc x is also convex on (0, \u03c0/4).\n\nSo, both functions are convex. Hmm. If both are convex, then applying Jensen to both sums might not directly help. Wait, the left-hand side is the sum of convex functions, and the right-hand side is also the sum of convex functions. But how can we compare them?\n\nAlternatively, maybe consider the difference: sec(2x) - csc x. If we can show that the sum of sec(2x) - csc x over x, y, z is non-negative, then the inequality holds. Let's see.\n\nLet me define h(x) = sec(2x) - csc x. Let's analyze this function on (0, \u03c0/4). If h(x) is convex or concave, maybe we can use some properties. But let's compute h(0) and h(\u03c0/4):\n\nAs x approaches 0+, sec(2x) approaches 1, csc x approaches infinity, so h(x) approaches -infinity. Not helpful. At x = \u03c0/4, sec(2x) = sec(\u03c0/2) which is undefined (infinite), 