ution, t = tan(A/2), and since A is acute, A/2 < \u03c0/4, so tan(A/2) < 1. So t \u2208 (0, 1).\n\nLet me define f(t) = (1 + t\u00b2)/(1 - t\u00b2) - sqrt(1 + t\u00b2)/t. We can check if f(t) \u2265 0 for t \u2208 (0, 1).\n\nCompute f(t):\n\nLet me factor sqrt(1 + t\u00b2):\n\nsqrt(1 + t\u00b2) [ (sqrt(1 + t\u00b2))/(1 - t\u00b2) - 1/t ]\n\nBut not sure. Let me compute f(t):\n\nf(t) = (1 + t\u00b2)/(1 - t\u00b2) - sqrt(1 + t\u00b2)/t\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator by 1:\n\nLet me compute a common denominator: t(1 - t\u00b2)\n\nThus,\n\nf(t) = [ t(1 + t\u00b2) - sqrt(1 + t\u00b2)(1 - t\u00b2) ] / [ t(1 - t\u00b2) ]\n\nLet me factor sqrt(1 + t\u00b2):\n\n= [ sqrt(1 + t\u00b2) [ t sqrt(1 + t\u00b2) - (1 - t\u00b2) ] ] / [ t(1 - t\u00b2) ]\n\nHmm, let me compute the numerator:\n\nLet me denote N = t(1 + t\u00b2) - sqrt(1 + t\u00b2)(1 - t\u00b2)\n\nFactor sqrt(1 + t\u00b2):\n\nN = sqrt(1 + t\u00b2) [ t sqrt(1 + t\u00b2) - (1 - t\u00b2) ]\n\nLet me set s = sqrt(1 + t\u00b2), so s \u2265 1, and t = sqrt(s\u00b2 - 1). Then:\n\nN = s [ sqrt(s\u00b2 - 1) * s - (1 - (s\u00b2 - 1)) ]\n\n= s [ s sqrt(s\u00b2 - 1) - (2 - s\u00b2) ]\n\nThis seems more complicated. Maybe another approach.\n\nAlternatively, square both sides of f(t) \u2265 0. Wait, but f(t) can be positive or negative. Let me check for t approaching 0 and t approaching 1.\n\nAs t approaches 0+, (1 + t\u00b2)/(1 - t\u00b2) approaches 1, and sqrt(1 + t\u00b2)/t approaches infinity. So f(t) approaches -infty. So f(t) is negative near 0.\n\nAt t = 1/\u221a3 \u2248 0.577, which corresponds to A = \u03c0/3, f(t) = 0, as we saw.\n\nAt t approaching 1-, (1 + t\u00b2)/(1 - t\u00b2) approaches infinity, sqrt(1 + t\u00b2)/t approaches sqrt(2)/1 = sqrt(2). So f(t) approaches infinity. So f(t) crosses zero somewhere between t=0 and t=1. Hence, f(t) \u2265 0 for t \u2265 t0, and \u2264 0 for t \u2264 t0, where t0 is the solution to f(t) = 0, which is t0 = 1/\u221a3.\n\nTherefore, in the equilateral case, all t = 1/\u221a3,