e calculation checks out.\n\nAlternatively, think of all possible ways to split the 3 weak teams into two groups. The probability that group A gets at least two is the same as the probability that group B gets at most one. But since the distribution is symmetric between A and B, the probability that A has at least two is the same as the probability that B has at least two. However, since there are only three weak teams, the events \"A has at least two\" and \"B has at least two\" are mutually exclusive, except when A has exactly two and B has one, but wait, if A has two, B has one, so neither has at least two. Wait, no, A has two, B has one. So, if A has at least two (two or three), then B has at most one (since total three). Therefore, the probability that A has at least two is equal to the probability that B has at most one, which is complementary to B having at least two. But since the setup is symmetric between A and B, the probability that A has at least two is equal to the probability that B has at least two. However, since these two probabilities can't both happen unless there are four weak teams, which we don't have here, their sum is equal to the probability that either A or B has at least two. But in our case, since there are only three weak teams, the events are actually mutually exclusive. If A has at least two, then B has at most one, so B cannot have at least two. Therefore, P(A \u22652) + P(B \u22652) = P(A \u22652 or B \u22652). But since if one has at least two, the other has at most one. However, in reality, the only way for either A or B to have at least two weak teams is that exactly one of them has two or three. Since total is three. So, the total probability is P(A \u22652) + P(B \u22652) = P(A \u22652) + P(A \u22641) = 1. Wait, no, that's not right. Wait, no. If we think of P(B \u22652) = P(A \u22641), since B is the complement of A. Because if group B has at least two, then group A has at most one. Therefore, P(A \u22652) + P(B \u22652) = P(A