plit already includes one group with at least two. The 3-0 split is another case where one group has three (at least two). The 0-3 split is the same as 3-0. So, actually, all possible splits except when both groups have less than two weak teams. But since there are three weak teams, it's impossible for both groups to have less than two. Because the total is three, so the minimum a group can have is zero, and the other three. So, actually, no matter how you split, one group will have at least two or the other will. Wait, no. Wait, if you split 2-1, one group has two, the other has one. So, the group with two has at least two, the other has one. So, in all splits, except when all three are in one group, but even then, that group has three. Wait, actually, in any split of three weak teams into two groups, one group must have at least two. Because 3 divided into two groups can only be 0-3,1-2,2-1,3-0. In all cases, one group has at least two. Wait, 0-3: one group has three, which is at least two. 1-2: one group has two. 2-1: same as 1-2. 3-0: same as 0-3. Therefore, actually, the probability that either group has at least two weak teams is 1. But that contradicts our previous thought.\n\nWait, but that can't be. Wait, if you have three weak teams, and you split them into two groups, then yes, one group must have at least two. Because the maximum number a group can have without reaching two is one. But three weak teams can't be split into two groups both having one, because 1+1=2 <3. Therefore, one group must have at least two. Therefore, the probability that either group A or B has at least two weak teams is 1. But this contradicts our previous calculation where for question 2, the probability that group A has at least two is 1/2. \n\nBut there's a key difference here. The first part of this thought is about the probability that either group has at least two, which is indeed 1. But the second question is specifically about group A having at least two. So, even though one of the groups must have at least two, the probability that it's specifically group A is 1/2, which makes sense. Because there's symmetry between A and B, so the probability that group A is the one with at least two is 1/2. But since one of them must have at least two, then the probability that group A has at least two is 1/2. So, this aligns with our previous calculation.\n\nTherefore, the answer to the second question is indeed 1/2.\n\nBut let's make sure once more by considering specific numbers. Suppose we have three weak teams: W1, W2, W3, and five strong teams: S1, S2, S3, S4, S5. We need to compute the number of ways to choose four teams for group A such that at least two are weak.\n\nThe total number of ways is C(8,4)=70. The number of favorable ways is:\n\n- Exactly two weak: C(3,2)*C(5,2)=3*10=30.\n\n- Exactly three weak: C(3,3)*C(5,1)=1*5=5.\n\nTotal:35.\n\nTherefore, 35/70=1/2. Yes, that's correct.\n\nSo, to recap:\n\n1. Probability that one group has exactly two weak teams: 6/7.\n\n2. Probability that group A has at least two weak teams: 1/2.\n\nThese are the results. Let me just check once more for any miscalculations.\n\nFor the first question, when we consider either group A or B having exactly two weak teams, since it's the same as the split being 2-1 or 1-2. The number of favorable splits is C(3,2)*C(5,2) + C(3,1)*C(5,3)=30+30=60. Total splits 70, so 60/70=6/7. Correct.\n\nFor the second question, group A having at least two weak teams: exactly two or three. That's 30+5=35. 35/70=1/2. Correct.\n\nTherefore, the answers are 6/7 and 1/2.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\n1. The probability is \\boxed{\\dfrac{6}{7}}.\n\n2. The probability is \\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{2}}.", "deepseek_solution": "Given that out of 8 teams, there are 3 weak teams, these 8 teams are divided into two groups \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) with 4 teams in each group by drawing lots.\n\n1. **The probability that one of the groups \\(A\\) or \\(B\\) has exactly two weak teams.**\n\n   - The total number of ways to divide 8 teams into two groups of 4 is \\(\\binom{8}{4} = 70\\).\n   - The number of favorable outcomes where one group has exactly two weak teams and the other has one is calculated as follows:\n     - Choosing 2 weak teams out of 3 and 2 strong teams out of 5 for group \\(A\\): \\(\\binom{3}{2} \\binom{5}{2} = 3 \\times 10 = 30\\).\n     - Choosing 1 weak team out of 3 and 3 strong teams out of 5 for group \\(A\\) (which is equivalent to group \\(B\\) having 2 weak teams): \\(\\binom{3}{1} \\binom{5}{3} = 3 \\times 10 = 30\\).\n   - Total favorable outcomes: \\(30 + 30 = 60\\).\n   - The probability is \\(\\frac{60}{70} = \\frac{6}{7}\\).\n\n2. **The probability that group \\(A\\) has at least two weak teams.**\n\n   - The number of favorable outcomes where group \\(A\\) has at least two weak teams (either 2 or 3 weak teams):\n     - Choosing 2 weak teams out of 3 and 2 strong teams out of 5: \\(\\binom{3}{2} \\binom{5}{2} = 3 \\times 10 = 30\\).\n     - Choosing all 3 weak teams and 1 strong team: \\(\\binom{3}{3} \\binom{5}{1} = 1 \\times 5 = 5\\).\n   - Total favorable outcomes: \\(30 + 5 = 35\\).\n   - The probability is \\(\\frac{35}{70} = \\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\n### Final Answer\n\n1. The probability is \\boxed{\\dfrac{6}{7}}.\n2. The probability is \\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{2}}.", "ground_truth_solution": "\nConsider a scenario where there are 8 teams with 3 of them being weak teams. We are to split the 8 teams into two groups \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) with each group consisting of 4 teams. We need to determine probabilities related to distributing these 3 weak teams.\n\n#### Part (1): Probability that one of the groups has exactly two weak teams\n\n1. ### Total number of ways to split the teams:\n   - The total number of ways to split 8 teams into two groups of 4 can be calculated using combinations:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total ways to select 4 teams out of 8} = \\binom{8}{4}\n   \\]\n   - Since the remaining 4 teams automatically go to the other group, we don't need to arrange subsets further. Additionally, dividing by 2 accounts for \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) being interchangeable:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total number of ways} = \\frac{\\binom{8}{4}}{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. ### Weak teams in one group:\n    - To have all 3 weak teams in one group and one additional team, we select 1 more team from remaining 5 teams:\n    \\[\n    \\text{Ways to select 1 non-weak team} = \\binom{5}{1} = 5\n    \\]\n\n3. ### Substituting and calculating:\n   - The total number of ways to split the teams such that all 3 weak teams go into the same group:\n     \\[\n     5 \\text{ total scenarios for all weak teams in one group}\n     \\]\n   - Probability that all 3 weak teams are in one group:\n     \\[\n     P_0 = \\frac{5}{\\frac{\\binom{8}{4}}{2}} = \\frac{5}{35} = \\frac{1}{7}\n     \\]\n\n4. ### Conclusion of Step (1):\n   - Probability that either group \\( A \\) or \\( B \\) has exactly 2 weak teams:\n     \\[\n     P = 1 - P_0 = 1 - \\frac{1}{7} = \\frac{6}{7}\n     \\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{6}{7}}\n\\]\n\n#### Part (2): Probability that group \\( A \\) has at least two weak teams:\n\n1. ### Symmetry:\n    - The problem states that \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) each might have at least two weak teams with the same probability, given either meets the requisite condition.\n    - Considering symmetry, suppose \\( E \\) represents the event where either group has two weak teams:\n    \\[\n    P(E_A \\cup E_B) = 1\n    \\]\n    - Since \\( E_A \\) and \\( E_B \\) are mutually exclusive, and both have the same probability:\n    \\[\n    P(E_A \\text{ or } E_B) = P(E_A) + P(E_B) = 1\n    \\]\n    - Hence:\n    \\[\n    P(E_A) = P(E_B) = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 1 = \\frac{1}{2}\n    \\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find the greatest four-digit number where all digits are distinct, and which is divisible by each of its digits. Zero cannot be used.", "deeps