 \\) range.\n\n**Conclusion**: The squirrel can jump to intercept the nut at \\( t = 0.5 \\, \\text{s} \\), when the nut is closest and within its jumping capability.\n\n**Answer**:  \n\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}", "ground_truth_solution": "1. **Problem Analysis and Initial Setup:**\n   - The problem involves a squirrel on a branch at a distance of \\(3 \\text{ m} 75 \\text{ cm}\\) (which is \\(3.75\\) meters) from Gabriel.\n   - Gabriel throws a nut horizontally at a velocity of \\(5 \\text{ m/s}\\) in the direction of the squirrel.\n   - The squirrel can jump with a great speed in any direction for a distance of \\(1 \\text{ m} 80 \\text{ cm}\\) (which is \\(1.8\\) meters).\n   - We need to check if the squirrel can catch the nut, ignoring air resistance.\n\n2. **Equations of Motion:**\n   - Coordinates of the squirrel initially:\n     \\[\n     x = a = 3.75 \\text{ m}, \\quad y = 0\n     \\]\n   - The nut\u2019s motion in time \\(t\\):\n     \\[\n     x(t) = V_{0} t, \\quad y(t) = \\frac{g t^2}{2}\n     \\]\n     where \\(V_{0} = 5 \\text{ m/s}\\) and \\(g = 10 \\text{ m/s}^2\\).\n\n3. **Distance Calculation:**\n   - The distance squared between the squirrel and the nut at time \\(t\\) is:\n     \\[\n     r^2 = \\left(V_{0} t - a\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{g t^2}{2}\\right)^2\n     \\]\n   - Substituting given values:\n     \\[\n     r^2 = \\left(5 t - 3.75\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{10 t^2}{2}\\right)^2\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     r^2 = \\left(5 t - 3.75\\right)^2 + \\left(5 t^2\\right)^2\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     r^2 = \\left(5 t - 3.75\\right)^2 + 25 t^4\n     \\]\n\n4. **Expanding and Combining Terms:**\n   - Expand \\(\\left(5 t - 3.75\\right)^2\\):\n     \\[\n     \\left(5 t - 3.75\\right)^2 = 25 t^2 - 2 \\times 5 t \\times 3.75 + 3.75^2\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     = 25 t^2 - 37.5 t + 14.0625\n     \\]\n   - Thus,\n     \\[\n     r^2 = 25 t^4 + 25 t^2 - 37.5 t + 14.0625\n     \\]\n\n5. **Rewriting in a Convenient Form:**\n   - Group the terms together:\n     \\[\n     r