ections to coincide at a single point.\n\nAnother angle: Let\u2019s consider the set of all intersection points of red lines. Each such point has a blue line passing through it. Similarly, the set of all intersection points of blue lines each have a red line passing through them. If we can show that all these intersection points are the same point, then all lines must pass through that point.\n\nSuppose there are two distinct intersection points of red lines, P and Q. Then there are blue lines l and m passing through P and Q respectively. If l and m intersect at point O, then any other intersection point of red lines must lie on a blue line passing through O. Otherwise, it would create another intersection of blue lines requiring a red line, leading to an infinite chain. Therefore, all blue lines must pass through O, and all red intersections must lie on blue lines through O, implying all red lines pass through O.\n\nWait, let's formalize this:\n\n1. Assume there are at least two red lines, which intersect at point P. There must be a blue line l passing through P.\n\n2. Suppose there is another pair of red lines intersecting at point Q \u2260 P. There must be a blue line m passing through Q.\n\n3. If l and m are distinct, they intersect at some point O.\n\n4. Now, consider any other intersection point R of two red lines. There must be a blue line passing through R. Let\u2019s call this blue line n.\n\n5. If n is distinct from l and m, then n intersects l at some point S and m at some point T. These intersections S and T are intersections of two blue lines, so there must be red lines passing through S and T.\n\n6. This creates new red lines, which intersect existing red lines, creating new intersection points that require blue lines, and so on.\n\nTo avoid this infinite regress, all blue lines must pass through O. Therefore, O is the common intersection of all blue lines.\n\n7. Since every intersection of red lines (like P, Q, R) must lie on a blue line, and all blue