em's condition, a blue line \\( l \\) passes through \\( P \\).\n   - Similarly, if there are two blue lines, they intersect at a point \\( Q \\), and a red line \\( m \\) must pass through \\( Q \\).\n\n3. **Inducing a Common Intersection Point:**\n   - Suppose there are two distinct intersection points of red lines, \\( P \\) and \\( Q \\). Each has a blue line \\( l \\) and \\( m \\) passing through them, respectively.\n   - Lines \\( l \\) and \\( m \\) (blue) intersect at a point \\( O \\).\n\n4. **All Blue Lines Pass Through \\( O \\):**\n   - Any other intersection \\( R \\) of two red lines requires a blue line \\( n \\) through \\( R \\). If \\( n \\) doesn't pass through \\( O \\), it intersects \\( l \\) and \\( m \\) at new points \\( S \\) and \\( T \\), necessitating red lines through \\( S \\) and \\( T \\). This infinite regress forces all blue lines to pass through \\( O \\).\n\n5. **Red Lines Must Pass Through \\( O \\):**\n   - Every intersection of two red lines lies on a blue line through \\( O \\). If a red line doesn't pass through \\( O \\), it intersects each blue line at distinct points not on \\( O \\), violating the condition. Hence, all red lines must pass through \\( O \\).\n\n6. **Conclusion:**\n   - All blue lines pass through \\( O \\) (from Step 4), and all red lines pass through \\( O \\) (from Step 5). Thus, all lines concur at \\( O \\).\n\n**Final Answer:**\nAll lines must intersect at a single common point. Therefore, under the given conditions, every line passes through this point, proving that all lines are concurrent. \n\n\\(\\boxed{\\text{All lines are concurrent}}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Assumption and Initial Observation:**\n\n   Consider $n$ lines in the plane, each colored either red or blue. According to the given conditions:\n   - No two lines are parallel.\n   - For any point of intersection of two lines of the same color, there exists a line of the other color passing through it.\n\n   We need to show 