ounded by \\( M \\), then \\( a + 4 \\leq M \\).\n   - For \\( x = 2 \\), \\( f\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right) = 2 + a^2 \\). This implies \\( 2 + a^2 \\leq M \\).\n   - Combining these, \\( 2 + a^2 \\leq M \\) and \\( a + 4 \\leq M \\). However, solving \\( M \\geq 2 + M^2 \\) leads to a contradiction since the quadratic \\( M^2 - M + 2 \\leq 0 \\) has no real roots.\n\nThus, the assumption that such a bounded function \\( f \\) exists leads to a contradiction. Therefore, no such function can exist.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{No}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Initial Assumptions and Definitions**:\n   Suppose there exists a real-valued function \\( f \\) on the reals such that:\n   \\[\n   f(x) \\text{ is bounded, } f(1) = 1 \\text{ and } f \\left( x + \\frac{1}{x^2} \\right) = f(x) + f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right)^2 \\text{ for all non-zero } x.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Least Upper Bound**:\n   Let \\( c \\) be the least upper bound of the set of values of \\( f(x) \\). Since \\( f(x) \\) is bounded, such a \\( c \\) exists. \n\n3. **Calculating \\( f(2) \\)**:\n   Using the functional equation for \\( x = 1 \\):\n   \\[\n   f \\left( 1 + \\frac{1}{1^2} \\right) = f(1) + f \\left( \\frac{1}{1} \\right)^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   f(2) = f(1) + f(1)^2 = 1 + 1^2 = 2.\n   \\]\n   Hence, \\( f(2) = 2 \\), implying \\( c \\geq 2 \\).\n\n4. **Finding \\( f(y + 1/y^2) \\)**:\n   By the definition of \\( c \\) as least upper bound, for any \\(\\epsilon > 0\\), there exists \\( y \\) such that:\n   \\[\n   f(y) > c - \\epsilon.\n   \\]\n   Particularly, let \\( \\epsilon = \\frac{1}{4} \\), then:\n   \\[\n   f(y) > c - \\frac{1}{4}.\n   \\]\n   Using the functional equation for this \\( y \\):\n   \\[\n   f \\left( y + \\frac{1}{y^2} \\right) = f(y) + f \\left( \\frac{1}{y} \\right)^2,\n   \\]\n   and since \\( f(x) \\leq c \\):\n   \\[\n   c \\geq f \\left( y + \\frac{1}{y^2} \\right) = f(y) + f \\left( \\frac{1}{y} \\right)^2.\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\( f(y) \\):\n   \\[\n   c \\