vide by 2:14x +15y=136\n\nAgain, take modulo14:15y\u2261136 mod14. 15 mod14=1, 136 mod14: 14*9=126, 136-126=10. So:\n\n1*y \u226110 mod14 => y\u226110 mod14. So y=10 +14k. Since y\u22651 and 15y \u2264136 -14=122 (if x\u22651). 15y \u2264122 => y \u22648.13. So y=10 is too big. Hence, no solution here.\n\nCase3: z=5\n\n28x +30y +155=365 =>28x +30y=210\n\nDivide by 2:14x +15y=105\n\nModulo14:15y\u2261105 mod14. 15\u22611, 105 mod14=105-14*7=105-98=7. So:\n\n1*y \u22617 mod14 => y\u22617 mod14. So y=7 +14k. Since y\u22651 and 15y \u2264105 -14=91 (if x\u22651). So 15y \u226491 => y\u22646.06. So y=7 is already too big. Thus, y=7 is not possible here. Hence, no solution for z=5.\n\nWait, but wait, y=7 would lead to 15*7=105, so 14x=105 -15*7=105 -105=0. But x must be at least1. So x=0 is invalid. So no solution here.\n\nCase4: z=7\n\n28x +30y +217=365 =>28x +30y=148\n\nDivide by2:14x +15y=74\n\nModulo14:15y\u226174 mod14. 15\u22611, 74 mod14=74-14*5=74-70=4. So y\u22614 mod14. So y=4 +14k. Since y\u22651 and 15y \u226474 -14=60 (if x\u22651). So 15y \u226460 => y\u22644. Thus, y=4 is the only possibility.\n\nCheck y=4:\n\n14x +15*4=74 =>14x +60=74 =>14x=14 =>x=1. So x=1, which is valid. So here's a solution: z=7, y=4, x=1. That's the real-world solution. So that's valid.\n\nCase5: z=9\n\n28x +30y +279=365 =>28x +30y=86\n\nDivide by2:14x +15y=43\n\nModulo14:15y\u226143 mod14. 15\u22611, 43 mod14=43-14*3=43-42=1. So y\u22611 mod14. So y=1 +14k. Since y\u22651 and 15y \u226443 -14=29 (if x\u22651). So 15y \u226429 => y\u22641.93. So y=1.\n\nCheck y=1:\n\n14x +15=43 =>14x=28 =>x=2. So x=2, y=1, z=9. Let's check: 28*2 +30*1 +31*9=56 +30 +279=365. Yes, that works. So this is another solution.\n\nWait, so there are at least two solutions: (1,4,7) and (2,1,9). Let me verify the second one: 28*2 is 56, 30*1 is 30, 31*9 is 279. Sum: 56+30=86, 86+279=365. Correct.\n\nSo z=9 gives another solution. So there are multiple solutions. Hence, the answer is yes, such natural numbers exist.\n\nBut wait, let's confirm if these are the only possible z values. We considered z=1,3,5,7,9. For each odd z from 1 to9. For z=7 and z=9 we found solutions, others didn't work. So there are at least two solutions. But the problem only asks if there exist natural numbers, so even one would suffice. But since we found two, that's even better.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there are more. Let me check z=11. Wait, earlier we said z\u22649 because 31*11=341, and 341 +28 +30=399 >365. So z=11 would require x and y to be at least1, making total 28+30+31*11=28+30+341=399>365. So z cannot be 11. So z=1,3,5,7,9 are the only possibilities.\n\nSo, in total, two solutions: (x,y,z)=(1,4,7) and (2,1,9). Let's see if there are more. Let's check z=7 and z=9.\n\nFor z=7, we had y=4, x=1.\n\nIs there another y for z=7? Wait, when z=7, the equation was 14x +15y=74. We found y=4. Let's see if there are other y's. y=4 +14k. But y=4+14=18 would require x to be negative. So no. So only y=4.\n\nSimilarly, for z=9, y=1. If we take y=1 +14=15, then 15*15=225, 14x=43 -225= -182, which is invalid. So only y=1.\n\nTherefore, only two solutions.\n\nBut wait, let me check another approach. Suppose we fix x and y instead. Let's see.\n\nAlternatively, let's model this as a linear Diophantine equation. The equation is 28x +30y +31z=365. Since we have three variables, it's a bit more complex, but with natural number constraints.\n\nBut perhaps we can think of it as 28x +30y =365 -31z. So for each z from 1 to 11 (but as above, z up to9), compute 365 -31z and see if it can be expressed as 28x +30y.\n\nBut since we already did that approach and found two solutions, maybe that's sufficient.\n\nAlternatively, think of it as coins: 28,30,31 denominations, can we make 365 with at least one of each. But that's similar.\n\nAlternatively, since 28,30,31 are all close to 30, approximate 30(x + y + z) \u2248365. Then x + y + z \u224812.166. So around 12. Which matches the real-world case of 12 months. So maybe x + y + z=12? Let's check.\n\nIn the first solution:1+4+7=12.\n\nSecond solution:2+1+9=12. So yes, both sum to12. So maybe the equation x + y + z=12 is also part of the system? Let me see:\n\nIf we assume x + y + z=12, then we can set up the system:\n\n28x +30y +31z=365\n\nx + y + z=12\n\nSubtract the second equation multiplied by28 from the first:\n\n(28x +30y +31z) -28(x + y + z)=365 -28*12\n\nWhich is 2y +3z=365 -336=29\n\nSo we get 2y +3z=29. Now we need to solve this with y, z \u22651 and x=12 -y -z \u22651. So y + z \u226411.\n\nSolve 2y +3z=29 for y, z \u22651.\n\nLet\u2019s express y=(29 -3z)/2. Since y must be integer, 29 -3z must be even. So 3z must be odd, meaning z is odd. So z=1,3,5,7,9.\n\nCheck possible z:\n\nz=1: y=(29-3)/2=26/2=13. Then x=12 -13 -1= -2. Invalid.\n\nz=3: y=(29-9)/2=20/2=10. x=12 -10 -3= -1. Invalid.\n\nz=5: y=(29-15)/2=14/2=7. x=12 -7 -5=0. Invalid (x must be \u22651).\n\nz=7: y=(29-21)/2=8/2=4. x=12 -4 -7=1. Valid. This is the first solution.\n\nz=9: y=(29-27)/2=2/2=1. x=12 -1 -9=2. Valid. This is the second solution.\n\nz=11: y=(29 -33)/2= -4/2=-2. Invalid.\n\nSo only z=7 and z=9 give valid solutions. Hence, confirming the two solutions we found earlier. Thus, the only natural number solutions are (1,4,7) and (2,1,9).\n\nTherefore, the answer is yes, there exist natural numbers x, y, z that satisfy the equation, such as x=1, y=4, z=7 and x=2, y=1, z=9.\n\nBut wait, the problem didn't ask for all solutions, just existence. So even one solution would suffice. Since we have at least one, the answer is yes.\n\nAnother way to think about this is that since the equation is inspired by the number of days in a year, and in reality, we have exactly that combination (1 February, 4 months with 30 days, 7 months with 31 days), it's a valid solution. The other solution (2,1,9) might correspond to a different distribution, like having two Februaries, which isn't realistic in a calendar year, but mathematically, it's still a valid solution with natural numbers.\n\nIn conclusion, yes, there are natural numbers x, y, and z that satisfy the equation. The solutions are found by systematically testing possible values of z (since z must be odd) and solving for y and x accordingly, leading to valid natural number solutions.", "deepseek_solution": "**Step-by-Step Explanation and Answer:**\n\nTo determine if natural numbers \\( x, y, z \\) satisfy \\( 28x + 30y + 31z = 365 \\), we analyze the equation systematically:\n\n1. **Real-World Context Insight**:  \n   Recognizing that 28, 30, and 31 are days in months, and 365 is the total days in a non-leap year, we recall the calendar distribution:  \n   - 1 month with 28 days (February),  \n   - 4 months with 30 days (April, June, September, November),  \n   - 7 months with 31 days (January, March, May, July, August, October, December).  \n\n   This gives \\( x = 1 \\), \\( y = 4 \\), \\( z = 7 \\). Verification:  \n   \\[\n   28(1) + 30(4) + 31(7) = 28 + 120 + 217 = 365.\n   \\]\n\n2. **General Mathematical Approach**:  \n   To confirm other possible solutions, we use modular arithmetic and substitution:  \n   - **Modulo 28 Analysis**:  \n     The equation reduces to \\( 2y + 3z \\equiv 1 \\mod 28 \\), implying \\( 3z \\) must be odd. Thus, \\( z \\) must be odd.  \n\n   - **Testing Odd \\( z \\) Values**:  \n     Possible \\( z \\) values: \\( 1, 3, 5, 7, 9 \\). For each \\( z \\), solve \\( 28x + 30y = 365 - 31z \\):  \n     - **\\( z = 7 \\):**  \n       \\( 28x + 30y = 148 \\Rightarrow 14x + 15y = 74 \\).  \n       Solving gives \\( y = 4 \\), \\( x = 1 \\).  \n     - **\\( z = 9 \\):**  \n       \\( 28x + 30y = 86 \\Rightarrow 14x + 15y = 43 \\).  \n       Solving gives \\( y = 1 \\), \\( x = 2 \\).  \n\n3. **Verification of Solutions**:  \n   - First solution: \\( (x, y, z) = (1, 4, 7) \\):  \n     \\[\n     28(1) + 30(4) + 31(7) = 365.\n     \\]  \n   - Second solution: \\( (x, y, z) = (2, 1, 9) \\):  \n     \\[\n     28(2) + 30(1) + 31(9) = 56 + 30 + 279 = 365.\n     \\]  \n\n**Conclusion**:  \nYes, there exist natural numbers \\( x, y, z \\) satisfying the equation. Valid solutions include \\( (1, 4, 7) \\) and \\( (2, 1, 9) \\).  \n\n**Final Answer**:  \n\\boxed{Yes}", "ground_truth_solution": "\nWe want to determine if there are natural numbers \\( x, y \\), and \\( z \\) that satisfy the equation \\( 28x + 30y + 31z = 365 \\).\n\n1. **Analyzing the Problem Context**:\n\n    To solve the problem, we first recognize that the equation \\( 28x + 30y + 31z = 365 \\) can be interpreted in the context of the days in a calendar year.\n\n2. **Identifying Parameters**:\n   \n    Let's recall the structure of a non-leap year:\n    - There are 12 months in a year.\n    - February has 28 days.\n    - Four months (April, June, September, November) have 30 days.\n    - The remaining seven months (January, March, May, July, August, October, December) have 31 days.\n\n3. **Assigning Corresponding Values**:\n   \n    We set:\n    - \\( x = 1 \\) for February (1 month of 28 days)\n    - \\( y = 4 \\) for the months with 30 days\n    - \\( z = 7 \\) for the months with 31 days\n\n4. **Verification**:\n   \n    Now, we need to calculate if these values \\( x, y \\), and \\( z \\) satisfy the equation:\n    \\[\n    28x + 30y + 31z = 365\n    \\]\n   \n    Substituting \\( x = 1 \\), \\( y = 4 \\), and \\( z = 7 \\) into the equation:\n   \n    \\[\n    28 \\times 1 + 30 \\times 4 + 31 \\times 7 \n    \\]\n   \n    Compute step-by-step:\n    \\[\n    28 \\times 1 = 28\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    30 \\times 4 = 120\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    31 \\times 7 = 217\n    \\]\n   \n    Adding these together:\n    \\[\n    28 + 120 + 217 = 365\n    \\]\n    \nThus, the left-hand side equals the right-hand side, confirming that the equation holds true.\n\n5. **Conclusion**:\n\nSince values \\( x = 1 \\), \\( y = 4 \\), and \\( z = 7 \\) satisfy \\( 28x + 30y + 31z = 365 \\), we conclude that such natural numbers \\( x, y \\), and \\( z \\) do exist.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given the function \\( y = \\frac{\\sqrt{4 - x^2}}{\\lg (|x| + x)} \\), determine its domain:\n(A) \\( [-2, 0) \\cup (0, 2] \\).\n(B) \\( \\left(0, \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\cup \\left(\\frac{1}{2}, 2\\right] \\).\n(C) \\( \\left[-2, \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\cup \\left(\\frac{1}{2}, 2\\right] \\).\n(D) \\( (0, 2] \\).\n\nTranslate to English: \nThe function \\( y = \\frac{\\sqrt{4 - x^2}}{\\lg (|x| + x)} \\) is given, determine its domain from the provided options:\n(A) \\( [-2, 0) \\cup (0, 2] \\).\n(B) \\( \\left(0, \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\cup \\left(\\frac{1}{2}, 2\\right] \\).\n(C) \\( \\left[-2, \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\cup \\left(\\frac{1}{2}, 2\\right] \\).\n(D) \\( (0, 2] \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out the domain of this function. The function is y equals the square root of (4 minus x squared) divided by the logarithm (base 10, I assume) of (absolute value of x plus x). So, the domain is all real numbers x where both the numerator and the denominator are defined, and the denominator isn't zero. Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, the numerator is sqrt(4 - x^2). For the square root to be defined, the expression inside must be greater than or equal to zero. So, 4 - x\u00b2 \u2265 0. Let me solve that inequality. Moving x\u00b2 to the other side, we get 4 \u2265 x\u00b2, which is the same as x\u00b2 \u2264 4. Taking square roots on both sides, that means x is between -2 and 2, inclusive. So the numerator is defined for x in [-2, 2].\n\nNow, the denominator is log(|x| + x). The logarithm function is only defined when its argument is positive. So, |x| + x > 0. Also, since log base 10 is used here, we need to ensure that |x| + x is not only positive but also not equal to 1, because log(1) is zero, which would make the denominator zero. Wait, but actually, the problem says to determine where the function is defined, so the denominator just needs to be non-zero and its argument must be positive. So first, let's handle |x| + x > 0, then check where |x| + x \u2260 1 to avoid division by zero. But maybe the denominator's argument being 1 is a separate consideration. Let me think. Wait, no, the denominator is log(|x| + x), so the denominator is defined when |x| + x > 0, and the denominator is zero when |x| + x = 1. So the function is undefined both when |x| + x \u2264 0 (since log isn't defined there) and when |x| + x = 1 (since denominator is zero). Therefore, we need to find x where |x| + x > 0 and |x| + x \u2260 1.\n\nBut let's first focus on where |x| + x > 0. Let's consider different cases based on the sign of x.\n\nCase 1: x \u2265 0. Then |x| = x, so |x| + x = x + x = 2x. For x \u2265 0, 2x > 0 when x > 0. So, for x > 0, this expression is positive. At x = 0, it's 0, which is not allowed. So, for x \u2265 0, |x| + x > 0 implies x > 0.\n\nCase 2: x < 0. Then |x| = -x, so |x| + x = -x + x = 0. So for any x < 0, |x| + x = 0. Therefore, |x| + x > 0 only when x > 0. So the domain for the denominator is x > 0.\n\nTherefore, the denominator log(|x| + x) is defined only when x > 0, and in that case, |x| + x = 2x, so the denominator becomes log(2x). So the denominator is log(2x), which is defined when 2x > 0, which again is x > 0, which we already have. Now, we also need to ensure that the denominator is not zero. So log(2x) \u2260 0. Since log(1) = 0, this happens when 2x \u2260 1, so x \u2260 1/2. Therefore, the denominator is zero when x = 1/2, so x cannot be 1/2.\n\nPutting it all together, the domain of the function is the set of x where:\n\n1. x \u2208 [-2, 2] (from the numerator's sqrt(4 - x\u00b2)),\n2. x > 0 (from the denominator's |x| + x > 0),\n3. x \u2260 1/2 (from the denominator's log(2x) \u2260 0).\n\nSo combining these, x must be in (0, 2], excluding x = 1/2. So the domain is (0, 1/2) \u222a (1/2, 2]. Let me check the options given.\n\nOption B is (0, 1/2) \u222a (1/2, 2], which matches. Option D is (0,2], which doesn't exclude 1/2. Option A includes negative numbers, but we saw that for x < 0, the denominator is zero (since |x| + x = 0), so log(0) is undefined, so those x are excluded. Option C is [-2, 1/2) \u222a (1/2, 2], but again, for x < 0, the denominator is undefined, so those x can't be included. Therefore, the correct answer should be B.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Let me verify each part again.\n\nFirst, numerator: sqrt(4 - x\u00b2) requires 4 - x\u00b2 \u2265 0 \u21d2 x\u00b2 \u2264 4 \u21d2 x \u2208 [-2, 2]. Correct.\n\nDenominator: log(|x| + x). As discussed, |x| + x > 0 only when x > 0. So denominator defined only for x > 0. Also, log(2x) \u2260 0 \u21d2 2x \u2260 1 \u21d2 x \u2260 1/2. So combining, x \u2208 (0, 2] excluding 1/2. Thus, (0, 1/2) \u222a (1/2, 2], which is option B. So yes, the answer should be B.\n\nBut wait, let me check the options again. The options are:\n\n(A) [-2, 0) \u222a (0, 2]\n\n(B) (0, 1/2) \u222a (1/2, 2]\n\n(C) [-2, 1/2) \u222a (1/2, 2]\n\n(D) (0, 2]\n\nSo yes, B is the correct one. But let me think again if there's any mistake. For example, when x is in (0, 1/2), 2x is between 0 and 1, so log(2x) is negative. Wait a second, log(2x) is negative when 2x < 1, i.e., x < 1/2. So the denominator would be negative for x in (0, 1/2), but the function is still defined there, as log can take negative values; it's just that the function's value would be negative. Wait, but the problem is just about the domain, not the range. So even if the denominator is negative, as long as it's defined and non-zero, it's acceptable. So log(2x) is defined for x > 0, and log(2x) \u2260 0 when x \u2260 1/2. So x in (0, 1/2) \u222a (1/2, 2] is correct. So yes, option B is correct.\n\nBut wait, another thing: when x is exactly 1/2, denominator is log(1) which is 0, so it's excluded. So x must be in (0, 2] except 1/2. Therefore, the domain is (0, 1/2) union (1/2, 2], which is option B. So I think that's right. Let me check if there's any other considerations.\n\nWait, but what about x=2? At x=2, the numerator is sqrt(4 - 4) = 0. Denominator is log(4), which is defined and non-zero (since 4 \u2260 1). So x=2 is allowed. Similarly, x approaching 0 from the right: numerator approaches sqrt(4) = 2, denominator approaches log(0+) which is log approaching 0 from the right, which would be negative infinity. Wait, but log(2x) when x approaches 0 from the right, 2x approaches 0, so log(2x) approaches -infty. Therefore, the function approaches 0 as x approaches 0 from the right, but x=0 is excluded because denominator is log(0), undefined. So x can approach 0 but can't be 0. So the interval is (0, 2], excluding 1/2. Therefore, answer B is correct.", "deepseek_solution": "The function \\( y = \\frac{\\sqrt{4 - x^2}}{\\lg (|x| + x)} \\) requires determining its domain by analyzing the conditions under which both the numerator and denominator are defined and non-zero.\n\n1. **Numerator Analysis**:  \n   The expression \\(\\sqrt{4 - x^2}\\) is defined when \\(4 - x^2 \\geq 0\\), leading to \\(x \\in [-2, 2]\\).\n\n2. **Denominator Analysis**:  \n   The denominator \\(\\lg(|x| + x)\\) must satisfy two conditions:  \n   - **Definition**: \\(|x| + x > 0\\).  \n     - For \\(x \\geq 0\\), \\(|x| + x = 2x > 0\\) implies \\(x > 0\\).  \n     - For \\(x < 0\\), \\(|x| + x = 0\\), which is invalid.  \n     Thus, the denominator is defined only for \\(x > 0\\).  \n   - **Non-Zero**: \\(\\lg(|x| + x) \\neq 0\\).  \n     Since \\(\\lg(1) = 0\\), we require \\(|x| + x \\neq 1\\). For \\(x > 0\\), this simplifies to \\(2x \\neq 1\\), so \\(x \\neq \\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\n3. **Combining Conditions**:  \n   - From the numerator: \\(x \\in [-2, 2]\\).  \n   - From the denominator: \\(x > 0\\) and \\(x \\neq \\frac{1}{2}\\).  \n\n   Intersecting these intervals gives \\(x \\in (0, 2]\\) with \\(x \\neq \\frac{1}{2}\\). This results in the domain \\((0, \\frac{1}{2}) \\cup (\\frac{1}{2}, 2]\\).\n\n**Final Answer**:  \n\\(\\boxed{B}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo determine the domain of the function \\( y = \\frac{\\sqrt{4-x^2}}{\\log (|x| + x)} \\), we need to consider the conditions where both the numerator and the denominator are defined and the denominator is not zero.\n\n1. **Numerator Analysis**:\n\n   The numerator is \\( \\sqrt{4 - x^2} \\). For a square root to be defined, the expression inside the square root must be non-negative:\n   \\[\n   4 - x^2 \\geq 0\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( x \\):\n   \\[\n   4 \\geq x^2 \\implies x^2 \\leq 4 \\implies -2 \\leq x \\leq 2\n   \\]\n\n2. **Denominator Analysis**:\n\n   The denominator is \\( \\log (|x| + x) \\). For the logarithm to be defined, the argument must be positive:\n   \\[\n   |x| + x > 0\n   \\]\n   This can be split into two cases depending on the value of \\( x \\):\n\n   - If \\( x \\geq 0 \\), then \\( |x| = x \\), so:\n     \\[\n     x + x > 0 \\implies 2x > 0 \\implies x > 0\n     \\]\n   - If \\( x < 0 \\), then \\( |x| = -x \\), so:\n     \\[\n     -x + x = 0\n     \\]\n     which does not satisfy the condition because it is not greater than zero.\n\n   Therefore, the valid range from this condition is \\( x > 0 \\).\n\n3. **Avoiding Division by Zero**:\n\n   We also need to ensure that the denominator does not equal zero:\n   \\[\n   \\log (|x| + x) \\neq 0\n   \\]\n   This condition translates to the argument of the log not being one (since \\( \\log 1 = 0 \\)):\n   \\[\n   |x| + x \\neq 1\n   \\]\n   - If \\( x > 0 \\), then the condition simplifies to:\n     \\[\n     x + x \\neq 1 \\implies 2x \\neq 1 \\implies x \\neq \\frac{1}{2}\n     \\]\n\n4. **Combining All Conditions**:\n\n   Summarizing the valid range of \\( x \\):\n   - From the square root condition: \\( -2 \\leq x \\leq 2 \\)\n   - From the logarithm's argument being positive: \\( x > 0 \\)\n   - From avoiding zero in the denominator: \\( x \\neq \\frac{1}{2} \\)\n\n   Combining these, we get:\n   \\[\n   0 < x < \\frac{1}{2} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad \\frac{1}{2} < x \\leq 2\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the domain of the function is:\n   \\[\n   \\left(0, \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\cup \\left( \\frac{1}{2}, 2 \\right]\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{B}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "690. Find the criterion for divisibility by 2 in a numeral system with any odd base.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, so I need to figure out the divisibility rule for 2 in a numeral system with an odd base. Hmm, let me start by recalling how divisibility rules work in different bases. In the decimal system, which is base 10 (even), the rule for divisibility by 2 is straightforward: check if the last digit is even. But the question here is about an odd base. Since the base itself is odd, the usual even-odd digit approach might not apply directly. Let me think.\n\nFirst, let's remember that in any base \\( b \\), a number is represented as a sum of digits multiplied by powers of \\( b \\). For example, a number \\( N \\) in base \\( b \\) with digits \\( d_n d_{n-1} \\ldots d_1 d_0 \\) is \\( N = d_n \\times b^n + d_{n-1} \\times b^{n-1} + \\ldots + d_1 \\times b + d_0 \\). To check divisibility by 2, we need \\( N \\mod 2 = 0 \\).\n\nSince the base \\( b \\) is odd, let's denote it as \\( b = 2k + 1 \\) for some integer \\( k \\). Now, let's analyze how each term \\( d_i \\times b^i \\) behaves modulo 2.\n\nLet's compute \\( b \\mod 2 \\). Since \\( b \\) is odd, \\( b \\equiv 1 \\mod 2 \\). Therefore, \\( b^i \\mod 2 = (1)^i \\mod 2 = 1 \\mod 2 \\). That means every term \\( d_i \\times b^i \\equiv d_i \\times 1 \\mod 2 \\equiv d_i \\mod 2 \\).\n\nTherefore, the entire number \\( N \\mod 2 \\) is equivalent to the sum of its digits modulo 2. Wait, so if I sum all the digits and check if the sum is even, then the number is divisible by 2? But wait, that seems too simple. Let me verify with an example.\n\nTake base 3 (which is odd). Let's take the number 12 in base 3. Converting to decimal: \\(1 \\times 3 + 2 = 5\\). 5 is not divisible by 2. Sum of digits is 1 + 2 = 3, which is odd. So according to the rule, since the sum is odd, the number is not divisible by 2. Correct.\n\nAnother example: number 11 in base 3. Decimal: 1\u00d73 +1=4. 4 is divisible by 2. Sum of digits:1+1=2, which is even. So the rule works here.\n\nAnother example in base 5. Number 14 in base 5: 1\u00d75 +4=9. 9 is not divisible by 2. Sum of digits:1+4=5, which is odd. Correct. Number 22 in base 5: 2\u00d75 +2=12, which is divisible by 2. Sum of digits 2+2=4, even. Correct.\n\nWait, so the rule is that if the sum of the digits is even, then the number is divisible by 2 in an odd base? But that contradicts my initial thought that the last digit might matter. Wait, but in decimal (base 10), which is even, the rule is about the last digit. But in an odd base, since the base itself is congruent to 1 mod 2, each power is 1 mod 2, so each digit contributes its own value mod 2. Therefore, the total sum mod 2 is the sum of digits mod 2. Therefore, the entire number's parity is the sum of its digits' parities. Hence, the divisibility by 2 depends on whether the sum of digits is even.\n\nBut wait, in base 3, the digits can be 0,1,2. Summing digits: 0+1+2=3 (for a 3-digit number). But each digit's contribution is mod 2, so evenness is determined by the count of odd digits? Wait, no. Each digit's value mod 2 is either 0 or 1. So sum of digits mod 2 is the same as the number of 1s mod 2 (if digits can only be 0 or 1). But in odd bases, digits can go up to base-1, which is even. So digits can be 0,1,...,b-1. For example, in base 5, digits go up to 4. So digits can be even or odd. So each digit's parity (even or odd) contributes to the total sum's parity.\n\nThus, the rule would be: a number is divisible by 2 in an odd base if and only if the sum of its digits is even. Because each digit's contribution mod 2 is equivalent to its own parity, and since the base is odd (so each position's weight is 1 mod 2), the total is sum of digits mod 2.\n\nBut let me check another example to be sure. Take base 7. Number 16 in base 7: 1\u00d77 +6=13, which is odd. Sum of digits 1+6=7, which is odd. Correct. Number 24 in base 7: 2\u00d77 +4=18, which is even. Sum 2+4=6, even. Correct. What about a number with more digits? Like 123 in base 7: 1\u00d749 +2\u00d77 +3=49+14+3=66, which is even. Sum 1+2+3=6, even. Correct. Another example: 135 in base 9: 1\u00d781 +3\u00d79 +5=81+27+5=113. 113 is odd. Sum 1+3+5=9, odd. Correct.\n\nTherefore, it seems the divisibility rule for 2 in an odd base is that the sum of all digits must be even. That's the criterion. So, the answer is that a number is divisible by 2 in an odd base if the sum of its digits is even. Therefore, the criterion is the sum of its digits is divisible by 2.\n\nBut let me think again. Is there any case where this might not hold? Suppose we have a digit that's even or odd. For example, in base 3, digit 2 is even. So if a number ends with 2, but sum of digits is odd, like 22 in base 3: 2\u00d73 +2=8, which is even. Sum 2+2=4, even. Correct. Another case: 112 in base 3: 1\u00d79 +1\u00d73 +2=14. Sum 1+1+2=4, even. 14 is even. Correct. What about 111 in base 3: 1\u00d79 +1\u00d73 +1=13, odd. Sum 1+1+1=3, odd. Correct.\n\nSo it seems consistent. Therefore, the divisibility rule for 2 in an odd base is that the sum of the digits must be even. That's interesting because in even bases, like base 10, it's only the last digit that matters because the base is divisible by 2, so all higher place values are multiples of 2, hence only the last digit's parity affects the total. But in odd bases, every place contributes 1 mod 2, so all digits contribute equally to the parity.\n\nTherefore, the criterion is that the sum of all digits must be even. Hence, the answer is that a number in an odd base is divisible by 2 if and only if the sum of its digits is divisible by 2.", "deepseek_solution": "To determine the divisibility criterion for 2 in a numeral system with an odd base \\( b \\), consider the following analysis:\n\n1. **Representation in Base \\( b \\):**  \n   A number \\( N \\) in base \\( b \\) with digits \\( d_n d_{n-1} \\ldots d_1 d_0 \\) is expressed as:  \n   \\[\n   N = d_n \\cdot b^n + d_{n-1} \\cdot b^{n-1} + \\ldots + d_1 \\cdot b + d_0.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Odd Base Property:**  \n   Since \\( b \\) is odd, \\( b \\equiv 1 \\mod 2 \\). Therefore, all powers of \\( b \\) satisfy \\( b^i \\equiv 1 \\mod 2 \\). This simplifies the expression for \\( N \\mod 2 \\):  \n   \\[\n   N \\mod 2 \\equiv \\left( d_n + d_{n-1} + \\ldots + d_1 + d_0 \\right) \\mod 2.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Divisibility Criterion:**  \n   For \\( N \\) to be divisible by 2, \\( N \\mod 2 = 0 \\). From the simplification above, this occurs if and only if the **sum of all digits** of \\( N \\) is even.  \n\n**Examples in Odd Bases:**  \n- **Base 3:** The number \\( 12_3 \\) (decimal 5) has a digit sum \\( 1 + 2 = 3 \\), which is odd \u2192 not divisible by 2.  \n- **Base 5:** The number \\( 22_5 \\) (decimal 12) has a digit sum \\( 2 + 2 = 4 \\), which is even \u2192 divisible by 2.  \n- **Base 7:** The number \\( 123_7 \\) (decimal 66) has a digit sum \\( 1 + 2 + 3 = 6 \\), which is even \u2192 divisible by 2.  \n\n**Final Answer:**  \nA number in an odd base \\( b \\) is divisible by 2 **if and only if the sum of its digits is even**.  \n**Criterion:** The sum of all digits must be divisible by 2.", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Let's denote the base of the numeral system as \\( d \\), where \\( d \\) is an odd number.\n   \n2. Consider any number in this numeral system:\n   \\[\n   x = \\overline{a_1 a_2 a_3 \\ldots a_{n-1} a_n} = a_1 \\cdot d^{n-1} + a_2 \\cdot d^{n-2} + a_3 \\cdot d^{n-3} + \\ldots + a_{n-1} \\cdot d + a_n\n   \\]\n\n3. To proceed, we will add and subtract the sum of the digits \\( a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + \\ldots + a_n \\). This is often referred to as the \"plus-minus technique\". \n\n4. An intermediate form of \\( x \\) can be obtained:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   x &= a_1 \\cdot d^{n-1} + a_2 \\cdot d^{n-2} + a_3 \\cdot d^{n-3} + \\ldots + a_{n-1} \\cdot d + a_n \\\\\n   &= \\left( a_1 \\cdot d^{n-1} - a_1 + a_1 \\right) + \\left( a_2 \\cdot d^{n-2} - a_2 + a_2 \\right) + \\left( a_3 \\cdot d^{n-3} - a_3 + a_3 \\right) + \\ldots + \\left( a_{n-1} \\cdot d - a_{n-1} + a_{n-1} \\right) + \\left( a_n \\right) \\\\\n   &= a_1 \\cdot (d^{n-1} - 1) + a_2 \\cdot (d^{n-2} - 1) + a_3 \\cdot (d^{n-3} - 1) + \\ldots + a_{n-1} \\cdot (d - 1) + (a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + \\ldots + a_n)\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n5. Here, we observe that since \\( d \\) is odd, each of the terms \\( d^{k} - 1 \\) where \\( k = n-1, n-2, \\ldots, 1 \\), will be even. Specifically:\n   \\[\n   d^{n-1} - 1, \\quad d^{n-2} - 1, \\quad \\ldots, \\quad d - 1\n   \\]\n   are all even.\n\n6. Therefore, all the terms involving \\( d \\) subtracted by 1 are divisible by 2.\n\n7. It follows that the number \\( x \\) is divisible by 2 if and only if the sum of its digits \\( a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + \\ldots + a_n \\) is divisible by 2.\n\n### Conclusion:\nA number in a numeral system with an odd base \\( d \\) is divisible by 2 if and only if the sum of its digits is divisible by 2.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The number is divisible by 2 if and only if the sum of its digits is even.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Is there a natural number \\( n \\), and if so, what is it, such that the sum of the first \\( n \\) natural numbers is a three-digit number with identical digits?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find a natural number n such that the sum of the first n natural numbers is a three-digit number with all identical digits. Let me start by recalling the formula for the sum of the first n natural numbers. I think it's S = n(n + 1)/2. Yeah, that sounds right. So we need S to be a three-digit number where all the digits are the same. Examples of such numbers are 111, 222, 333, up to 999. So essentially, S can be 111, 222, ..., 999. So first step is to set up the equation n(n + 1)/2 = ddd, where d is a digit from 1 to 9. So ddd is 111*d, right? Because 111*1=111, 111*2=222, etc. So the equation becomes n(n + 1)/2 = 111*d, where d is an integer between 1 and 9. So 111*d must be equal to n(n + 1)/2. Therefore, n(n + 1) = 222*d. So n(n + 1) must be 222 times some digit d from 1 to 9. So first, maybe I can check for each d from 1 to 9 whether 222*d can be expressed as n(n + 1). Let me note that n and n + 1 are consecutive integers, so they are coprime. Therefore, their product is 222*d. Since they are coprime, one of them must be a divisor of 222*d and the other must be a divisor of 222*d divided by that. Hmm, this might be a bit abstract. Maybe a better approach is to compute 222*d for each d and check if it's a product of two consecutive integers. Let's list out 222*d for d from 1 to 9: For d=1: 222*1=222 For d=2: 222*2=444 For d=3: 222*3=666 For d=4: 222*4=888 For d=5: 222*5=1110 For d=6: 222*6=1332 For d=7: 222*7=1554 For d=8: 222*8=1776 For d=9: 222*9=1998 Now, each of these numbers (222, 444, 666, 888, 1110, 1332, 1554, 1776, 1998) must be equal to n(n + 1). So n(n + 1) is a product of two consecutive integers. Let's check each of these numbers to see if they can be expressed in such a way. Starting with d=1: 222. Let's solve n^2 + n - 222 = 0. Using the quadratic formula: n = [-1 \u00b1 sqrt(1 + 888)]/2 = [-1 \u00b1 sqrt(889)]/2. sqrt(889) is approximately 29.816, so n \u2248 ( -1 + 29.816 ) / 2 \u2248 28.816 / 2 \u224814.408. Not an integer. So d=1 is not possible. Next, d=2: 444. Equation: n^2 + n - 444 = 0. Discriminant: 1 + 1776 = 1777. sqrt(1777) \u2248 42.16. So n \u2248 (-1 + 42.16)/2 \u2248 41.16/2 \u224820.58. Not integer. d=3: 666. Equation: n^2 +n -666=0. Discriminant:1 + 2664=2665. sqrt(2665)\u224851.62. So n\u2248(-1+51.62)/2\u224850.62/2\u224825.31. Not integer. d=4:888. Equation: n^2 +n -888=0. Discriminant:1 + 3552=3553. sqrt(3553)\u224859.60. So n\u2248(-1 +59.60)/2\u224858.60/2\u224829.3. Close to 29.3. Not integer. d=5:1110. Equation: n^2 +n -1110=0. Discriminant:1 +4440=4441. sqrt(4441)\u224866.64. So n\u2248(-1+66.64)/2\u224865.64/2\u224832.82. Not integer. d=6:1332. Equation: n^2 +n -1332=0. Discriminant:1 +5328=5329. Wait, 5329 is a perfect square. sqrt(5329)=73. So n=(-1 +73)/2=72/2=36. So n=36. Let me check: sum from 1 to 36 is 36*37/2=666. Wait, 36*37/2= (36/2)*37=18*37=666. But 666 is a three-digit number with identical digits (all 6s). So here, d=6. So this works. Wait, but wait, in our previous list, for d=6, 222*6=1332. But n(n+1)=1332. But here, n=36, n+1=37, 36*37=1332. Yes, that's correct. So n=36 gives sum 666, which is a three-digit number with identical digits. So that works. Let's check the other values to see if there are more solutions. d=7:1554. Equation: n^2 +n -1554=0. Discriminant:1 +6216=6217. sqrt(6217)\u224878.85. So n\u2248(-1 +78.85)/2\u224877.85/2\u224838.92. Not integer. d=8:1776. Equation: n^2 +n -1776=0. Discriminant:1 +7104=7105. sqrt(7105)\u224884.3. So n\u2248(-1 +84.3)/2\u224883.3/2\u224841.65. Not integer. d=9:1998. Equation: n^2 +n -1998=0. Discriminant:1 +7992=7993. sqrt(7993)\u224889.4. So n\u2248(-1 +89.4)/2\u224888.4/2\u224844.2. Not integer. So only for d=6, we get an integer solution n=36. Wait, but let's verify. If n=36, the sum is 36*37/2=666. Yes, which is 666. All digits are 6. So that's a valid solution. Are there any others? From the above checks, it seems not. But wait, let me check d=3 again. For d=3, 666, which would be n(n+1)=666. Wait, n^2 +n -666=0. We saw that sqrt(2665)\u224851.62, but 51.62 squared is about 2665. So 51.62 is approximately sqrt(2665). So (51.62)^2\u22482665. So n\u224825.31. Wait, but 25*26=650, 26*27=702. 650 and 702. 666 is in between, so no integer solution. Similarly, for d=4: 888. n\u224829.3. Check n=29: 29*30=870. n=30:30*31=930. 870 and 930. 888 is in between, so no integer solution. Similarly, for d=5:1110. n\u224832.82. Check 32*33=1056. 33*34=1122. 1056 and 1122. 1110 is in between. Not a product of consecutive integers. For d=7:1554. 38*39=1482. 39*40=1560. 1482 and 1560. 1554 is in between. For d=8:1776. 41*42=1722. 42*43=1806. 1722 and 1806. 1776 in between. For d=9:1998. 44*45=1980. 45*46=2070. 1980 and 2070. 1998 in between. So no solutions except for d=6. Therefore, the only natural number n is 36. Wait, but let me think again. Maybe there's another approach. The sum S = n(n +1)/2 = 111*d. So 111*d must be a triangular number. 111 factors into 3*37. So n(n+1)/2 = 3*37*d. Therefore, n(n+1) = 2*3*37*d = 6*37*d. Since 37 is a prime number, and n and n+1 are coprime, one of them must be a multiple of 37. Let me suppose that n = 37*k. Then n+1 =37*k +1. Then their product is 37*k*(37*k +1) = 6*37*d. Divide both sides by 37: k*(37*k +1) =6*d. So k*(37k +1) must equal 6*d. Since d is between 1 and 9, 6*d is between 6 and 54. So k*(37k +1) must be between 6 and 54. Let's try k=1: 1*(37*1 +1)=1*38=38. 38=6*d \u21d2 d=38/6\u22486.333. Not an integer. k=2: 2*(37*2 +1)=2*75=150. 150=6*d \u21d2 d=25. But d must be \u22649. So not possible. So k=1 gives d\u22486.333, which is not integer. So maybe n+1 is a multiple of 37. Let's assume n+1=37*m. Then n=37*m -1. Then n(n+1)=(37*m -1)*37*m =37*m*(37*m -1)=6*37*d. Divide both sides by 37: m*(37*m -1)=6*d. Again, m*(37m -1) must be between 6 and 54. Let's try m=1: 1*(37*1 -1)=1*36=36. 36=6*d \u21d2 d=6. That works! So m=1 gives d=6. Then n=37*m -1=37*1 -1=36. Which is the solution we found earlier. Let's try m=2: 2*(37*2 -1)=2*73=146. 146=6*d \u21d2d\u224824.333. Too big. Similarly, m=0 is invalid because m must be at least 1. So the only possible solution here is m=1, leading to n=36 and d=6. Therefore, this confirms that n=36 is the only solution. Alternatively, another way to approach it is to factor the equation. Since n and n+1 are coprime, one must be a multiple of 37 and the other must be a multiple of 6*d/37. Wait, but 37 is a prime. So either n or n+1 must be divisible by 37. Let's suppose n=37k. Then n+1=37k +1, which would need to be equal to (6*d)/k. Wait, this might not be straightforward. Alternatively, since n and n+1 are coprime, and 37 is a prime factor in the product n(n+1)=222*d=2*3*37*d. Therefore, 37 divides either n or n+1. So as before, either n=37k or n+1=37k. If n=37k, then n+1=37k +1. Then the product is 37k*(37k +1)=2*3*37*d. Cancel 37: k*(37k +1)=6*d. As before, since k and 37k +1 are coprime (since consecutive numbers can't share factors other than 1), then k must divide 6*d. But since k*(37k +1)=6*d and d \u22649, k must be small. Testing k=1 gives 38=6*d \u21d2d=38/6\u22486.333, not integer. k=2 gives 2*75=150=6*d\u21d2d=25, too big. So invalid. If n+1=37k, then n=37k -1. Then product is (37k -1)*37k=6*37*d. Cancel 37: k*(37k -1)=6*d. Then for k=1:1*36=36=6*d\u21d2d=6. So valid. k=2:2*73=146=6*d\u21d2d\u224824.333, invalid. Therefore, only k=1 gives a valid d=6. Hence n=37*1 -1=36. Therefore, the only solution is n=36. So after checking all possible d values from 1 to 9, and analyzing the equation through factoring and considering divisibility by 37, the only natural number n that satisfies the condition is 36. Let me just confirm once more: sum from 1 to 36 is 36*37/2=666. Yes, 666 is a three-digit number with all digits identical. Therefore, the answer is n=36.", "deepseek_solution": "The sum of the first \\( n \\) natural numbers is given by \\( S = \\frac{n(n + 1)}{2} \\). We need \\( S \\) to be a three-digit number with identical digits, i.e., \\( S = 111d \\) where \\( d \\in \\{1, 2, \\ldots, 9\\} \\). This leads to the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n + 1)}{2} = 111d \\implies n(n + 1) = 222d.\n\\]\n\nWe analyze \\( 222d \\) for \\( d = 1, 2, \\ldots, 9 \\):\n\n- **For \\( d = 6 \\):**  \n  \\( 222 \\times 6 = 1332 \\). Solving \\( n(n + 1) = 1332 \\):  \n  \\[\n  n^2 + n - 1332 = 0 \\implies n = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 + 4 \\times 1332}}{2} = \\frac{-1 \\pm 73}{2}.\n  \\]  \n  The positive root is \\( n = 36 \\).  \n  Verification: \\( \\frac{36 \\times 37}{2} = 666 \\), which is a three-digit number with identical digits.\n\n- **For other values of \\( d \\):**  \n  Solving \\( n(n + 1) = 222d \\) yields non-integer roots or values of \\( d \\) exceeding 9. For example:  \n  - \\( d = 3 \\): \\( 222 \\times 3 = 666 \\), but \\( n \\approx 25.31 \\) (not an integer).  \n  - \\( d = 4 \\): \\( 222 \\times 4 = 888 \\), but \\( n \\approx 29.3 \\) (not an integer).  \n\n**Key Factorization Insight:**  \nSince \\( n \\) and \\( n + 1 \\) are coprime, one must be divisible by 37 (a prime factor of 222). Testing \\( n + 1 = 37k \\):  \n- For \\( k = 1 \\), \\( n = 36 \\), leading to \\( S = 666 \\), which satisfies the condition.\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nThe only natural number \\( n \\) such that the sum of the first \\( n \\) natural numbers is a three-digit number with identical digits is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{36}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n#### 1. First Solution:\n\nConsider the sum of the first $n$ natural numbers, which is given by the formula:\n\\[\nS = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\]\nWe need to find if there exists an $n$ such that $S$ is a three-digit number where all digits are the same. Such numbers take the form $111k$ where $k = 1, 2, \\ldots, 9$. Therefore, we need to solve the equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2} = 111k\n\\]\n\nMultiplying both sides by $2$ to clear the fraction:\n\\[\nn(n+1) = 222k\n\\]\n\nRearranging this, we get the quadratic equation:\n\\[\nn^2 + n - 222k = 0\n\\]\n\nSolving for $n$ using the quadratic formula, $n = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}$, where $a = 1$, $b = 1$, and $c = -222k$:\n\\[\nn = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 + 888k}}{2}\n\\]\n\nFor $n$ to be a natural number, the expression under the square root, $1 + 888k$, must be a perfect square. Let:\n\\[\n1 + 888k = m^2 \\text{ for some integer } m\n\\]\n\nSince $m^2$ must be odd for $n$ to be an integer (as $n$ is a positive integer), we test feasible values for $k$. Testing for $k = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9$ while ensuring $1+888k$ is a perfect square, we find that:\n\nFor $k = 6$,\n\\[\n1 + 888 \\cdot 6 = 5329 = 73^2\n\\]\n\nThus, substituting back for $n$:\n\\[\nn = \\frac{-1 + 73}{2} = 36\n\\]\n\nTherefore:\n\\[\n\\boxed{36}\n\\]\n\nis the $n$ such that the sum of the first 36 integers is a three-digit number with equal digits, specifically $666$.\n\n#### 2. Alternative Solution:\n\nStarting with:\n\\[\nn(n+1) = 222k\n\\]\n\nNoticing that:\n\\[\n0 < \\frac{n(n+1)}{2} < 1000\n\\]\nimplies\n\\[\n0 < n(n+1) < 2000\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\\[\n0 < n < 45\n\\]\n\nGiven the left side of the earlier equation, $n(n+1)$, must be divisible by 37:\n\\[\nn = 37 \\text{ or } n+1 = 37\n\\]\n\nIf $n = 37$, then $37 \\times 38$:\n\\[\n37 \\cdot 38 = 1406 \\quad \\text{(not of the form $222k$)}\n\\]\n\nIf $n + 1 = 37$, then:\n\\[\nn = 36\n\\]\n\nAgain confirming:\n\\[\n\\boxed{36}\n\\]\n\nis the solution found consistently above. This completes the calculation and result verification.", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Suppose the function \\( y= \\left| \\log_{2} \\frac{x}{2} \\right| \\) has a domain of \\([m, n]\\) and a range of \\([0,2]\\). What is the minimum length of the interval \\([m, n]\\)?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the minimum length of the interval [m, n] for the function y = |log\u2082(x/2)| where the domain is [m, n] and the range is [0, 2]. Let me start by understanding the problem step by step.\n\nFirst, let's recall that the domain of a function is the set of all possible input values (x-values) for which the function is defined. The range is the set of all possible output values (y-values) the function can take. Here, the function is given as the absolute value of a logarithm, so I should consider the properties of logarithmic functions and absolute value functions.\n\nThe function inside the absolute value is log\u2082(x/2). Let me break this down. The logarithm log\u2082(x/2) is defined when x/2 > 0, which means x > 0. However, since the domain is given as [m, n], m must be greater than 0. The absolute value will affect the range because it reflects any negative parts of the log function to be positive.\n\nThe range of the function y = |log\u2082(x/2)| is [0, 2]. That means the maximum value of y is 2, and the minimum is 0. Since the absolute value is involved, the original logarithm can take both positive and negative values, but the absolute value makes them all non-negative. Therefore, the expression inside the absolute value, log\u2082(x/2), can range from -2 to 2 because when we take the absolute value, the maximum becomes 2. Wait, let me check that.\n\nIf the range is [0, 2], then the original log\u2082(x/2) must have values such that when we take the absolute value, the highest value is 2. Therefore, log\u2082(x/2) can be as low as -2 and as high as 2. Because when log\u2082(x/2) is between -2 and 2, the absolute value would make it between 0 and 2. So that gives me two equations: log\u2082(x/2) = 2 and log\u2082(x/2) = -2. Let me solve these to find the corresponding x-values.\n\nStarting with log\u2082(x/2) = 2. To solve for x, I can rewrite this equation in exponential form. 2\u00b2 = x/2, so 4 = x/2, which implies x = 8.\n\nNext, log\u2082(x/2) = -2. Similarly, converting to exponential form: 2\u207b\u00b2 = x/2. That's 1/4 = x/2, so x = 2*(1/4) = 1/2.\n\nTherefore, the logarithm function log\u2082(x/2) reaches 2 at x = 8 and -2 at x = 1/2. But since we're taking the absolute value, the points where log\u2082(x/2) is negative (i.e., x < 2, since log\u2082(x/2) < 0 when x/2 < 1 \u21d2 x < 2) will correspond to y = |log\u2082(x/2)| = -log\u2082(x/2). So, the graph of y = |log\u2082(x/2)| will be V-shaped, with a vertex at x = 2 where the ori