alyze its critical points. Let me denote \\( g(x) = \\ln f(x) \\).\n\nSo:\n\n\\( g(x) = \\ln(1 + x^{2018}) + 2016 \\ln(1 + x) - 2017 \\ln(2x) \\).\n\nCompute derivative \\( g'(x) \\):\n\nFirst term derivative: \\( \\frac{2018 x^{2017}}{1 + x^{2018}} \\).\n\nSecond term derivative: \\( 2016 * \\frac{1}{1 + x} \\).\n\nThird term derivative: \\( -2017 * \\frac{1}{x} \\).\n\nThus,\n\n\\( g'(x) = \\frac{2018 x^{2017}}{1 + x^{2018}} + \\frac{2016}{1 + x} - \\frac{2017}{x} \\).\n\nWe need to analyze the sign of \\( g'(x) \\). If we can show that \\( g'(x) \\) changes sign appropriately, such that the minimum of \\( g(x) \\) is at \\( x = 1 \\), then since we know \\( g(1) = 0 \\), that would suffice.\n\nLet's compute \\( g(1) \\):\n\n\\( g(1) = \\ln(2) + 2016 \\ln(2) - 2017 \\ln(2) = (1 + 2016 - 2017) \\ln(2) = 0 \\). So that checks out.\n\nNow, check the derivative at \\( x = 1 \\):\n\n\\( g'(1) = \\frac{2018 * 1}{1 + 1} + \\frac{2016}{2} - 2017 * 1 = \\frac{2018}{2} + 1008 - 2017 \\).\n\nCompute:\n\n\\( 2018 / 2 = 1009 \\).\n\nSo, 1009 + 1008 - 2017 = (1009 + 1008) - 2017 = 2017 - 2017 = 0. So at \\( x = 1 \\), the derivative is zero, which suggests a critical point there.\n\nNow, to see if \\( x = 1 \\) is a minimum or maximum, let's check the second derivative or test intervals around \\( x = 1 \\).\n\nAlternatively, let's check the behavior of \\( g'(x) \\) when \\( x < 1 \\) and \\( x > 1 \\).\n\nFirst, consider \\( x \\) approaching 0 from the right.\n\nAs \\( x \\to 0^+ \\):\n\n- \\( \\frac{2018 x^{2017}}{1 + x^{2018}} \\approx 2018 x^{2017} \\to 0 \\).\n\n- \\( \\frac{2016}{1 + x} \\approx 2016 \\).\n\n- \\( \\frac{2017}{x} \\to \\infty \\).\n\nTherefore, \\( g'(x) \\approx 0 + 2016 - \\infty = -\\infty \\). So \\( g'(x) \\) is negative near 0.\n\nAs \\( x \\to \\infty \\):\n\n- \\( \\frac{2018 x^{2017}}{1 + x^{2018}} \\approx \\frac{2018 x^{2017}}{x^{2018}} = \\frac{2018}{x} \\to 0 \\).\n\n- \\( \\frac{2016}{1 + x} \\approx \\frac{2016}{x} \\to 0 \\).\n\n- \\( \\